— — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — CHAPTER 1 — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —
“Get up, Ember.” My friend Adva calls as she shakes me awake. “The camp is leaving! We'll be left behind.” 

The sun shines through the tent's fabric as Adva stands above me. A soft, yet urgent smile rises on her pale face as she sees my eyes open. Adva moves my burning hair from in front of my eyes. “You awake? 
Wonderful!” She yells quickly. As she moves, a slow and peaceful breeze follows her body haloed in clouds, circling around the tent. “Any slower and the war will have ended. Come on!” Adva jokes. She wears a
pack over her shoulder, stuffed with her clothes and valuables, including a device holding onto its last bit of life through a thin wire holding two separate parts together. I don’t know if it even still works.

I sit up, rubbing small sparks out of my eyes. Then I stretch, allowing my burning flames to grow as I raise my arms far above my head. The tent is fire-proof… no other option would suit a fire elemental like 
myself, after all. Adva stares at me. She's not usually one to repeat herself, her gazes always send the clearest messages anyway. I force myself through my drowsiness to stand to my feet, looking aimlessly for
my own pack. Mine contains similar contents to Advas, however lacking the obsolete devices. 

As I locate and pick my pack up, I finally hear the sounds of the mass exodus of the elementals outside our tent as they realize that they are also late. Adva crosses her arms, noticing that I finally understand
the urgency. “You ready now?” She asks, a small hint of sass in her voice. I nod quickly, throwing my pack over my shoulder. My eyelids are still heavy, but I have no time to wake myself up. 

I walk out of the tent, being greeted by a cold blood rain. My flames dampen in response to the moisture, now entering a battle against the environment to keep burning. The murky red rain evaporated against my 
skin once it managed to pierce my exo-flames, as my body was too hot for it to reach after I had just stretched. We Firen Elementals average pretty hot temperatures, no surprise. However when we exert ourselves, 
we can reach dangerous temperatures. We rapidly cool naturally, though, as such a temperature all day everyday would be disastrous and unlivable. 

Adva emerges from the tent behind me. As usual, a cool and slow breeze follows her steps. She reaches to grab my arm, but pulls her hand back when she feels the heat coming from my body. “Come on!” She says 
quickly, choosing words instead. “The entire camp is basically empty.” Adva’s Windsi, her small sentient breeze, pushes my body forward with urgency. They say that having a Windsi is a sign of immense strength 
—something Adva denies. I see the potential in her.

I would know—My father was a warrior. One of the best, in fact. As I stumble forward, his sparring manual thumps around in my pack — the last thing he gave me before he was drafted and forced to fight in the war
he had no business fighting in. They killed him out there. 

Adva grabs my wrist tightly after my body had cooled some. She began to do what's known as ‘Flux-Flying’, a form of transport where a soft-element ‘Flux’ Elemental raises themselves slightly above the ground, 
propelling themselves forward in a skating motion using their element for acceleration and balance. I can do this as well, but Adva’s superior speed is causing me to suffer. I struggled to keep balance, nearly
hitting the ground a few times. If this is her way of waking me up, it's certainly working. I immediately get overstimulated, being forced to focus on holding my bag to ensure it doesn't fall, to keep my feet 
steady so as to not fall, and to focus on not hitting the ground, all while trying to match her speed.

“Adva, slow down!” I yelp, raising my voice in an attempt to push more urgency. However, my demand is swiftly ignored, and Adva keeps her same, smooth pace while ignoring me. The wind sings as we fly, and my 
knees nearly slam into the blood-soaked grass as I trip. Exerting so much power in my lower body has heated it up significantly. I have to focus on regulating my wrists’ temperature so I don’t burn her. If only
she knew what I was doing for her. 

After what felt like hours, Adva finally slows down, allowing me to plant my feet and take some steps. My lower body has grown warm due to this extreme and panicked exertion, and the deep murky red plummeting
onto the grass quickly evaporates from around my feet. I’m happy that grass has grown resistant to the extreme temperatures of val. It gets annoying when you leave trails of fiery carnage where you walk. Adva 
continues pulling me, and voices of other elementals begin to pour from over the hill. 

There’s so many more than I expected, of multiple types. From all nations across the world, despite being within Naturine territory. The specific camp I was staying in was hiding from the Firen nation’s brutal
military by moving around frequently. I never thought it would work, Irision loves to propagate its military as omnipotent. 

Which is unfortunate, because we’re close to them. Truth be told, Irision is never far enough. It’s infamous for being the worst of the invaders. Ever since they marched on our capital, nobody’s gotten a full 
night’s rest. Our democracy was destroyed in favor of a radical nationalist ideology called Knightsdale, stripping everyday people of their freedoms in the name of total control.

“Keep your head low,” Adva mutters. “And stay close.” She tightens her grip on me and her ears flicker. I watch her eyes dart to every soul we pass. Her Windsi curls around us, bringing a peaceful zephyr of 
cool air with it.

As Adva and I weave through the tight crowd, I notice Firen Elementals branded with the Irision symbol. They deserted from the military, but you can only run so far on Tulia. They huddle together, isolated, as 
many opt to keep their distance… elementals only skirt around them, and one dares to spit at them. Despite the taunts from others, they still laugh together and one even draws in the blood soaked dirt. I wince 
at the sight of them. Some of them may have been forcefully drafted, but I can’t help but wonder how many civilians they’ve murdered.

Another group sits nearby, sheltered by a makeshift building. They’re commonly referred to as the Unfortunate. Peering through the open doors shows the horrors this place tries to hide—famine, disease, and 
injury, all cramped into one tiny building. Medics rush in and out of the building with food and herbs, trying valiantly to save every life they can. The sight of the unfortunate makes me shiver. I wish I could
help them, but I’m not too fortunate myself.

Adva mutters something under her breath. Her gaze is locked onto the overpacked building. She bows her head slightly towards them. Then, she speaks again. “I hope they are loved, protected, and healed…”

“As do I,” I respond, opting to forget about the speech I missed. “It’s no way to live.”

Adva sighs before shaking her head quickly, snapping back into shape. She returns to her suspicion, each step being drowned in questions. A radio in the distance screams loud. “Naturine soldiers appro—” it barks,
before the woman with it turns its volume down and pulls it to her ear. Naturine soldiers… my chest grows warm. That’s hope. Perhaps they’ll escort us.

Adva nudged my shoulder as a small smile spread across her face, breaking her demeanor —even just for a second. “Look.” She pointed toward a cluster of Firens and Naturine—singing, not fighting. Dancing. To the
Anthem of our nation, before it was poisoned. Despite the war, national pride stands strong for many, including myself. Firen Pride is something I’ll never lose. I can’t help but silently hum the song of my nation ...

“Live free and fly … the fire dances, the flame never dies … Tulia’s moons hold Iris close … for the Firen nation is a beacon of hope…”

They sing the hymn of the democracy that we lost. The new one crafted by the Knightsdale speaks of horrors that the world doesn’t utter. I will never sing it, nor will I bow to it. It took the nation I loved from me,
then had the audacity to take my parents, too. Yet it demands I kneel. Never.

Adva places her hand on my shoulder. “Calm, Ember…” her voice grows soft. “I didn’t mean to upset you.” 

“It’s… not your fault.” I turn to look over at her. After a pause, I comment “Don’t you think it’s a little weird?”

She tilts her head, her hair flowing with it. “Hmm? What’s weird?” She asked, her ears flickering again.

“They sit together… I haven’t seen Naturine dance with us since the war began.” 

“Perhaps everyone’s just happy.” Her gaze wandered away from the group to the other crowds. She was right—This place was remembering joy. Elements flew and moved like they did before the nightmare happened. Despite
the downpour of the murky blood rain, everyone felt free again. Anthems rang through the skies and old dances formerly banned for their underlying meaning were being taught to the youth who had it robbed from them. I 
couldn’t help but smile. 

“We should get moving again,” Adva said as she grabbed my wrist again. Her voice shook and her head spun… I felt the gazes of suspicion tearing through my spirit. I lower my chin to avoid the judgemental glares…

Adva suddenly stops. When I raise my head, I see why. Two international soldiers known as peacekeepers rest before us, in their uniforms which have been stained pink by the rain. I shiver as they scan me, eyes darting 
from head to toe. If I dare to make a sudden move I could very well be thrown onto the ground. They are equipped with multiple tools to restrain us if necessary, including ties to keep our wrists down and weapons to lower 
our energy.

“We’ve got 2 potential Knightsdale Youth”, said one of them. A male, with a deep yet clear voice, spoke into the microphone resting in the technology on his forearm. “Airren, Firen. Southside of the camp.” His hand hovered
over his restraints as he gazed at us.

“We’re processing them, tell them that.” The other one commented quickly. A soft, female voice, almost motherly. She approached Adva. An array of blue beams shot out from her own tech, and she scanned Adva up and down. Adva
was still, didn’t even blink. Hands at her side, feet together, and eyes staring blankly forward. 

The male sighed. “We can only process so many, Saea.” He shook his head as he approached me. 

“Would you rather leave innocent children trapped in this hellish war?” Saea begins to dig into Adva’s bag. “You got an ID on you, child?” 

Adva nods. “It’s underneath everything. I haven’t had to bring it out in a while, I apologize.” Her voice is emotionless and monotone. Saea began searching for it, emptying Adva’s bag, careful not to damage any of her stuff.

The man sighed as he approached me. I could hear him mumbling under his breath in a language I couldn’t understand before beginning to scan me as well. I mimic Adva’s stiffness. Last time we encountered peacekeepers, we 
nearly got detained for “talking too disrespectfully”. We have a different peacekeeper to thank, as they arrived and calmed their partner down. It was still a terrifying moment, though. 

Saea finds Adva’s ID, and begins typing into the large screen on her forearm. “When’s your birthday?” She asked, eyes locked onto her task. 

Her partner had finished scanning me. “Where’s your ID?” He asks, his voice sounding nearly as robotic as Adva's. 

“In my bag,” I shiver. I don’t remember if it’s actually in there. In fact, I don’t remember if I even have it. I try not to let my anxiety show. He goes onto to search my bag as well.

Saea nods. “She’s good.” She says to her partner, before typing even more in her forearm tech. I begin to wonder what those things do. 

“Nothing in here,” the man said from behind me. “Can’t find it anywhere.”

I freeze. “Wh-What?!” My mind scrambles. Where could it be? Could I have left it somewhere? Maybe it fell out while I was Flux-Flying. “I- I had it in there a while ago! It was there I- I swear!” I instantly glance to Adva
for help…

Saea extends her hand to me, snatching my gaze. “Calm, child.” Her voice is soft and motherly. My breathing has grown rapid as my mind races. What if they arrest me and take me to some detention center? I’ll be stuck on Tulia 
alone, forever! All because I forgot my ID! I can’t let that happen, no.. I can't stay on Tulia—I can’t let Adva go alone… what if they kill her on Gaea? What if—

Saea lays her hand on my head. Her Watrish flesh boils and I watch her wince, but she stands firm otherwise. “I’m not going to hurt you child,” she began. Her voice was softer than before. She extended her other hand, taking 
mine. Steam rises from her hand as our elements clash. “Just tell me your name, your age, and a family member’s name. I’ll make you a new one right now.”

The murky rain paired with my anxiety makes me shiver. I take a deep breath. “My name’s.. Ember.. Ember Hayes.” Saea motions to the man behind me, and I hear him begin typing on his own device. The woman rubs my hand with her 
thumb, creating even more steam. She bites down hard on her own jaw, as if trying to swallow a yelp, nodding as I speak. “I-.. turned forty a while ago. My father was.. Axor Hayes..” I swallow my anxiety. I just trust that the
worker behind me got it right… 

“Axor?” Saea half-smiles. “I loved watching his battles before this all happened… Do you have any proof that you’re his daughter?”

I nod quickly. “Yes, I do!” A smile grows across my face. She knows my father! He’d be so happy if he was here. “He gave me a copy of his book… It's personally signed by him with a wonderful note. Please be careful with it..”

The man was already holding my book. He flips through it, making a small Naturine shield from his arm  to protect it from the rain, all the way to the last page, and sure enough, there it is! My father’s note… It reads:

“My lovely daughter … I know how much you enjoy watching the daily arena battles, and your ambition to become a warrior like myself and our ancestors. I gift you this book for you to read so that you can eventually fulfill
that dream of yours … and give you something to remember me by when I travel and you can’t see me! … Just remember I will always love you, no matter how far I am … Sincerely, your father ♡” 

“It’s here.” Said the man. “It looks like his handwriting too.”

Saea nods. “You’re good then, Ember. Sorry for stressing you out.” She then begins to type into the tablet on her arm, and it prints me a fresh, new ID. She then hands it to me—has a picture of me and everything.

The weight of the anxiety had long left my chest, but it was still relieving to hear. “It’s alright… Thank you.” I smile. The peacekeepers walk off without another word, looking for more suspicious persons or nascent. 

Adva had been standing there silently. “... That was stressful.” She says as she approaches me. I know she wanted to help, but you don’t intervene with peacekeeping operations… the quickest way to get yourself into a detention
center… That, and she likely didn’t know what to say.

I exhale. I quickly check through my bag to make sure everything is in order, sliding the new ID in a secure pocket as I do. I don't want it to disappear again. “I’m okay,” I brush off my head. There’s lingering steam from Saea’s
hand on my forehead. “She did a good job calming me down…”

“Let’s just get to the group now.” Adva begins leading me forward. “I see them gathering… we don’t want to be late.” 

As we approach the growing crowd of elementals, some pass out suits to keep us safe in the hostile environment of space. Dying up there is arguably one of the most painful ways to die, as the vacuum expands you until you explode.
Not the way I want to die.

After we put our suits on, we joined a sprawling group near the flat plain. Already, the suit’s white has been diluted to a light pink in the bloody rain. Once we reach the group, we find ourselves walking under massive umbrellas
made from many different elements. Adva’s wind pet guides me along so I don’t lose her, leading us through the knot of elementals. I apologized to someone I brushed by habit, thinking I had burned them, forgetting the suit protects
them from my flames.

Weaving through the crowd, we eventually come across a rather sparse area filled with elementals around our age. Another struggle with the Knightsdale Youth: everyone avoids you like the plague. Knightsdale Youth are known to steal, 
injure, and kidnap to further their deeds. More peacekeepers are sweeping through this group of youth, giving them all suits as they pass. 

Adva and I rest under a nearby umbrella, which only one other silent young elemental rests beneath. He wears a bandage on his upper left arm, stained with blood. The rain slamming against it brings quite the soothing sound. Adva 
shifts, turns, and eventually just lays down. It’s hard to get comfortable in these suits. 

“Wonderful weather,” Adva jokes. “Couldn’t possibly be any better.” 

I giggle. “Agreed. Blood rain makes the best morning weather.”

The nascent on the other side of the umbrella lets out a low laugh. “... I’m not used to blood rain.” His voice is quite raspy, and he seems as if he hadn’t slept for a while. He’s a Snai elemental—Those who are one with the Snow. 

Adva glances, a distrustful gaze falling upon the Snai. “... Do we know you?” She asks. 

“No,” He whispered. “Nobody does. I prefer it that way.”

Adva glances at me. Admittedly, I feel bad when I see a nascent like this. They are alone, and I couldn’t imagine if I didn’t have Adva with me. I feel I’d be the same way, or worse. “Do you… have any friends?” I mutter,
recognizing after that my question was foolish… he had already said nobody knows him.

He sighed. “No. Friends are only helpful until they leave… then the burden they leave you with is unbearable.” 

I immediately assumed he meant death. It would explain his current demeanor and loneliness… but then again, I don’t want to overshoot his tragedy. But I don’t want to downplay it either… so I leave him with a silent nod of
understanding, clouding my confusion in a land he will hopefully never find. 

“Have you seen them?” He begins. He picks his head up to look at us. “The Coven soldiers. The ones who just… enjoy the thrill of making others feel fear.”

Adva and I just sit in silence. “So you have…” The Snai continues. “I watched one kill a child right in front of the mother.  For what?...”

Tears well in my eyes. How cruel does one have to be to tear away their offspring? Before their own eyes? Is this really what Tulia has come to? 

He turns his gaze away from us. Quiet whimpers come from his corner as he curls into a ball.

I can’t help but say something to try and lighten his mood. “Well, we’ll be safer on Gaea…”

“Ah.” he cracks a fake smile through his tears. “I unfortunately can’t go… I have no identification in the peacekeeper’s systems.”

“Oh,” Adva’s voice cracks. “I… am terribly sorry to hear that, friend. You—”

“Friend?” His head snapped over to her . “We are not friends quite yet… I haven’t even caught your names.”

“... We have not caught yours either.” Adva rebuts, crossing her arms as she stares at him.

The Snai chuckles. “That’s a fair point… friend.”

Naturine soldiers begin to approach the wider group from all sides. You can tell they’re official because they wave the national flag of the Naturine Army: A green background with black symbols of a spear with vines 
around its handle and the edges of the flag. They march in strong formations, with the front holding shields by their waists. That’s a good indicator that they have no violent intentions—That, and the fact that they’re
one of the Phalanx nations. They likely wouldn’t be attacking untrained refugees. 

I notice they begin to converse with peacekeepers. I can’t hear them from here, nor do I feel confident in any guesses I could come up with as to what they’re talking about. I look over to the other two, and they’ve
been attentive to the distant negotiations as well. 

“They’re… scared,” the stranger said. I watched him swallow his fear as his eyes darted around the group. “Look at their faces… Something’s coming.” 

Adva’s windsi stirs, zig zagging around us. He’s right. Something is coming, and we should probably get moving. As if on queue, the peacekeepers begin rushing towards the larger group as Naturine warriors take defensive
positions around nearby groups of youth. Adva gets up quickly, and the Snai elemental just sighs.

I jumped up. As the peacekeepers approach, they yell. “Diala! Diala! Take cover!”

Screams roar. Shields sprout, facing the skies. Civilians run, jump, duck, and dive. 

Adva uses her Airren control to latch onto the top of the umbrella we’re under, and pull it down. It gives us more cover from the sky… which is what Diala is—Death from the sky. Raining hellfire to cause as much chaos as possible.

“... How long do you think they’ll attack?” the nascent asked. I could see some of his snowy body beginning to melt as he quivered in fear.

“I’ll protect you,” Adva said without looking at him. “Just take deep breaths. Be calm, and do—”

BOOM. CRASH. SHATTER.

These sounds continued on… our umbrella shook with each strike the ground absorbed, but each passing moment, I watched it grow weaker. I instinctually went to grip the strap of my pack, but it’s underneath the suit.
It should be safe. The nascent began rocking back and forth, almost vibrating from terror. Adva and I lock eyes for a moment, figuring out what to do. 

“... My name’s Ember.” I crawl over to him. “Ember Hayes. What’s yours?”

He looks at me with weary, cautious eyes. Scans me for something. “Just call me Vael…”

I smile. “Vael it is. My friend there is Adva. It’s nice to meet you, friend…”

I see him make the beginnings of a smile on his face, right before a much louder explosion lands right on top of us. Our umbrella completely shatters, leaving us exposed to the incoming terror. The surrounding area is covered
in clouds of green, brown, black, and purple—The main sign of Poison air. The blood rain pierces it, but it does not do us the justice of clearing it.

Adva and I have on the suits to protect us from it, but Vael is completely suitless. He’ll die if he breathes too much of it in. Acting quickly, Adva jumps up and forms an air bubble around us. Her Windsi assists her in
keeping the poisonous air out, but I already hear her panting. 

“We have to get out of here!” Adva yells. But I can’t tell which way is ‘out’. The sounds of coughing, explosions, and screams blind my perception. My ears ring, my vision blurs, and I lose track of which way is forward.

Poisonous air begins to seep through small cracks in the bubble. Adva’s exhaustion is beginning to show, she pants and sweats as her element flickers around her. Her balance begins to falter and she almost falls, but Vael
catches her, helping her keep stable.

I stand on my feet and use my own element to help push the air away. The shaking ground throws me off my balance, but Vael also helps me stabilize by using his element to freeze my feet to the ground. It requires a lot of
maintenance from him, though, as my use of my element has caused my body to heat up, and my body now reaches a high enough temperature to melt through his efforts swiftly.

“This isn’t working…” he yelled. “We’re tiring ourselves… we have to move!” 

An explosion lands right outside Adva’s bubble, throwing us in an unknown direction. The good news is we are still together… but the bad news is Vael is now exposed to the toxic air, and I hear one of my oxygen tanks burst.

“Hold your breath!” I yell to Vael. I do the same. While this suit can detect and shut down oxygen tanks that have been compromised, it takes a few moments, so I’ve got no choice but to stop breathing. But as a Firen elemental,
I require far more oxygen, and I cannot hold my breath for nearly as long. My fiery organs burn through oxygen at a much faster rate than other elementals, and I cannot survive for nearly as long without breathing.

I see Adva struggling to stand as well. I can’t tell how she’s doing, but it looks like her suit is fine. I can tell she is injured in some way, as she’s holding her arm and moaning in pain. But if I don’t help Vael first, he
may just die. He has no suit, after all… he’s going to be breathing this in.

I jumped over to him. My body and suit are still smoldering hot, so I try not to touch him, but I can force some of the air away using flames. Fire burns through the poisonous air and clears it when expelled normally.. But when
it explodes in poisonous air, it causes a massive nightmarish explosion. Now that I think about it, that’s what they’re probably trying to do…

Vael crawls over to me quickly. He notices the hole in my oxygen reservoir and seals it with his element for me. Finally, the suit detects the compromised tank and seals it off. I can breathe again, but a little bit of the deadly
air may have snuck in.

“Thank you…” He utters through his low, raspy voice. I hardly hear him through all the screams and explosions.. But I can read his lips. I can tell in his eyes that  he’s definitely inhaled some of it… he needs help quickly.

Adva’s standing now—her arm is clearly hurt, but she’s still fighting. She limps towards Vael and I before forming a much weaker bubble than before. She’s resilient as always, but she’s growing more tired by the second. 

Her Windsi pushes us, guiding us in an unknown direction. It pushes us through the ever-growing clouds of poison. Vael trips and stumbles as he tries to keep up, and I have to grab his arm, burning him, in an attempt to
help up. His yelp pierces my heart, but I hope he can forgive me…

We begin to move, led by Adva and her windsi. Her groans from exhaustion and pain hurt my soul, but she leads us on strong. I use my ability to push the poison away to keep what breaks through- out, and Vael covers up
what he can with his snow. Here we are, surviving, with optimal teamwork and an unbreakable resolve to survive.

Then, the worst comes.

BOOM.

A large explosion far away to our left. The fiery-poison cloud grows high to the sky. Then, the chain reaction begins. The explosion expands just as a wildfire would. Adva picks up speed as her windsi urges haste.
We all begin to run, panting, in pain, exhausted, terrified for our lives. The terror of death grows ever louder, ever closer. I begin to reminisce. I think of my parents. My old friends. The Gods. Why us? Why me?
I don’t want to die…

It creeps. The sound crawls. The vision is haunting… bodies of those not as far as us fly up, and the terror of the possibility of that being us grips my heart. Just run, Ember. Just run. 

Suddenly, Saea showed up, wearing a gas mask. She immediately springs into action, sliding her way into Adva’s bubble and coating it in water. The new bubble grabs us, holding us within its tightly knit currents.
Then she glides us across the ground at triple the speed we were fleeing. But it’s still not fast enough.

The ground shakes more. Slowly but surely we lose our lead on it. Debris flies into our bubble. Saea picks up her speed, but it feels almost futile. Then, the bubble tightens. Something wraps around it—and then we
are thrown out of the danger. The death that felt so certain just a moment ago was now distant. The sound, while still loud, was not nearly as loud as it was moments ago. 

Once the water bubble collapses, there stands the Naturine male peacekeeper as well. He had used a massive vine to grab us and pull us to safety. “Medical! We have youth over here! Medical, please!” He yells. Two
Ballad Elementals run over. Ballad Elementals have the ability to control blood, and this makes them wonderful doctors and medics. They tend to Adva and Vael first, as they are the two most obviously injured. It’s
better that way.. They needed it most.

From here, I can see the demons in the sky who rained their hell down on us. Unfortunately, Naturine elementals cannot fly, as they are Core elementals. This means they are limited to defenses on the ground until
help arrives. Luckily for them, they are excellent at these ground defenses… when they have the time to get them prepared. Right now, it’s looking pretty grim. I do see them fighting back, sending vines upward, 
yanking the cowards out of the sky…

I suddenly just broke down into tears. I curl into a ball and my entire body shakes and quivers. I force my head out of the suit and immediately vomit some fiery instantiation of fear from the bottom of my stomach.
The tears—which are not water, but steam, as my body is incapable of producing any fluids beyond my yal and jal energies—cover my entire face in moments that felt like hours. My entire body hurts, when just a second
ago I felt just fine…

Everything felt so painful. Each drop of rain landing on my skin burned more than any flame could. Each blade of grass felt like a needle trying to pierce my skin. Every item in my pack touching me made me sensitive.
All of this combined simply made me weep more, it was too much to handle.

A medic rushes over to me. “Breathe, nascent. You live. You are safe. Breathe.”

My fire dampens and burns slower. My confusing emotions have caused my body to shut down, to focus on stabilizing everything else. I’m helpless, at the mercy of this medic and the environment. I can hardly move. I 
yell for Adva, my father, Saea, anyone to help me…

I feel my blood moving as it shouldn’t as the medic gets to work. It usually tickles, but right now it feels like a thousand small needles piercing each of my veins. I yelp in pain and bite my tongue, fighting to sit
still. My quivering body overwhelmed by sensation makes it difficult.

“You’re okay.” she utters over and over. Despite my cries, she doesn’t stop tending to my internal injuries. I slowly begin to feel better physically, but I’m still a wreck. Such a close encounter with death has broken
whatever spirits I had left. We’d never escape to Earth. We’d never be free. We’d never—

My mind halts. My body stops quivering and my anxiety flees. I feel peaceful. My breathing returns and my tears stop flowing. Everything’s okay. The medic had given me a sort of emotional sedative… to calm me and stop
my scrambling mind. Since I’m no longer thinking of my impending doom, it clearly worked. I look up at her… and she’s got a large smile on her face. 

“Feeling better, nascent?” She pats my forehead. I nod, but then I notice the tickly feeling in my veins. I can’t help but laugh… “Oh! I see it worked well on you…” The medic comments. “Sit up, tell me if anything hurts.”

I do as commanded, leaning forward and sitting up straight. I move my arms, twist my back, flourish my element a little, and I've got no pain. I feel fantastic… which, when thinking about how I felt moments ago… feels
kind of odd. The wonders of Tulian medicine, everyone. “I feel great.” I say, a small smile growing on my face. “Thank you.”

The medic patted my head again. The explosions had ended. Back up had arrived- Watrish elementals were in the air fighting off those raining the attack down on us. I don’t even remember why I was worried. The medic puts
her hands up in front of me, and holds up a finger. “Can you follow my finger, sweetie?” She asked. Then she begins to move her hand back and forth. I follow perfectly, moving my eyes with her finger. She stops and smiles
at me. “Very good. Does your head hurt at all?” I shake my head. “How about your lungs? Take a deep breath.” She lays her hand in the middle of my chest. “Does it hurt to breathe?” 

I take a really deep breath, holding it for a while, waiting for pain to pop up. Yet, nothing, but a bit of tension in my chest. I let the doctor know and she gave me one last head pat before moving onto others nearby.
I look around for Adva, who’s still getting treatment for her injuries, and Vael, who is laying in the grass staring at the ongoing battle in the sky. Watching the Poisi monsters that tried to murder us is oddly satisfying. 

I crawl over to Vael. He doesn’t move a muscle, as if he didn’t hear me approaching. I wave my hand above him, and he still doesn’t respond. “Hey,” I say. Nothing. “Vael!” I shake him. I just came to the conclusion that 
he was ignoring me… it was hard for me to even formulate a thought of something potentially worse. 

He turned his head over to me, looking sluggish and tired. “Hm?” His demeanor was much like mine, but he seemed very disinterested and lost. “What’s up?”

“Are you well?” I asked. It feels like I only asked that question out of courtesy, instead of genuine worry.

Vael nodded slowly. “I’m fine… I think.” He kept nodding as he spoke… but he eventually stopped to check if he was injured. He patted down his torso. “Yes. I am well.”

He sits up. “I don’t even remember being scared…” He whispers. “Why does this treatment work so well?”

“Thank the Gods it does,” I say. “I’d rather be this way than whatever I was a few moments ago.”

Vael’s eyes shift to me as he tilts his head. He just stares for a moment before looking back up at the sky.

The onslaught from the sky has long stopped. Attacking forces have scattered in response to the growing defensive presence. Now they work to clear out the smoke and debris, and save the few who survive. Another peacekeeper
approaches, standing directly in front of my line of sight to the former battlefield, with two new suits. “I noticed yours was broken,” he said. “Take this. And you, nascent, you need one too.” He placed new suits for Vael
and I in our laps, mine being slightly oversized…

The peacekeeper gave our heads light taps before walking off. Vael looked over at me. “I didn’t show him my ID, why did he give me one?”

“I don’t think he cared,” I responded, looking down at my new suit. “Perhaps he’s just trying to give them all out as fast as possible.”

A second large group is gathering, this time guarded by peacekeepers and Phalanx soldiers. Adva stands in between Vael and I, laying her hands on our heads. “I don’t think sitting here is going to get us to Earth guys.” Her
voice is as calm as mine, and yet she’s still got her signature sass. “Get up, get your suits on, and let’s get going.”

I stand to slip out of my current broken suit and jump into the new one. It’s far more spacious, drooping down its extra space onto itself. I can’t tell how I look but I can only imagine I look kind of silly.

Vael puts his on with Adva’s assistance. His is a much better fit than mine but he seems to struggle moving in his more than I do. Adva laughs when he almost falls. Regardless, we begin our march over to the new group. 
There’s hundreds of Phalanx soldiers in the skies patrolling now. They scout the skies from the ground, alongside air patrols weaving through the clouds. 

As we approach, I overhear two soldiers talking. 120 civilian deaths, and counting. Nothing new for the Coven. Anyone that isn’t a loyalist is an enemy, and all enemies must be killed remorselessly… just one of the many laws 
engrained in Knightsdale society. 

A voice booms louder and louder as we inch closer. “... This setback will not prevent our freedom! We will flee with Phalanx escort! Nothing shall stop us! …” I lose focus on the voice, as I can’t help but notice a small group
of soldiers also putting on suits. I rationalize that they must be escorting us, like the voice said. Cheers boom from the crowd, likely reacting to something I ignored. 

“I knew it would only get better,” Adva said through a giggle. Our medicine’s effects have begun to fade now, even though I can hear it lingering in her voice. I begin to slightly fixate on the near death experience again. In
all my years on Tulia, dodging the war, eating scraps of food, avoiding Coven patrols, never have I gotten that close to my demise. Safe to say, I never wish to experience that again. My fear of death has definitely gotten worse.

I looked over to check on Vael. He had tucked his hands beneath his arms, keeping his head low. I considered trying to talk to him, but whatever he was thinking about looked serious. I turned over to Adva who noticed it as 
well… we exchanged curious glances for a moment before she shrugged. I opted to simply lay my still dormant hand on his back to silently comfort him through whatever imprisoned his consciousness. 

We arrived at the new gathering. Elementals were noticeably standing further apart now, clearly incredibly hesitant after the attack. Peacekeepers and soldiers alike were sweeping through the crowds, giving everyone space suits 
and supplies for our flight. Luckily, I could feel my bag being intact. After all of that I would’ve expected some damage, which to be fair, I can’t properly check right now. My bag is under my suit, and I’d have to take it off
to look.

Nobody stops to check on us. We’ve all got suits that are undamaged, and don’t look injured at all. We’re low priority right now, and I think we could use the downtime, so it works out. 

Unfortunately, our downtime doesn’t last long. Fortunately, it’s because we’re officially getting off of this planet. The dormant suits begin to power up as sky patrols give go-aheads for take off. My excitement rises, but only 
temporarily. Tulia is a mess of war, death, and destruction, but it’s still my home. It’ll always be my home, and I’ll forever await the day that I return. 

Elementals of all kinds begin to take to the sky. The suit’s visor displays light shows of tutorials on how to properly fly using it. Then, it begins to resize itself, tightening around the lower leg area until it’s skin-tight.
These space suits we’ve created are genius—they use the val that elementals naturally exert as power, which allows them to stay alive for as long as the elemental needs. We’ve made even better ones that can turn val into sustenance,
but they’ve been confiscated for military use. So, we’re stuck with the downgrade.

Vael’s element, Snow, can’t fly fast enough to get off the planet. So, his suit links to mine, since I’m the closest, and a little rope comes out. Then, mine deploys one as well, and he links us together. After that, the ropes
tighten. “Don’t fly too fast, please…” he mumbled. “Not used to being airborne like you.”

I smile softly. “I’ll consider it.” I turn to Adva, who’s been standing patiently waiting. However I notice a bit of boredom on her face, so maybe she’s not as patient as I previously thought. I nod at her, and she prepares to fly.
I do as well.

The process of flight is different for every elemental. My mother taught me the basics of Firen flight, which led me to get my license in it at quite a young age. She herself was all too graceful in the air, always doing my 
favorite tricks for my birthday. She was drafted with my father, and killed along with him.

Adva’s patience has run out. She flies off into the air at a steady pace. Slow enough for us to keep our eyes on her, but fast enough to make me feel a sense of urgency. I boost off the planet quickly, hearing Vael’s yelps behind
me. I look to see him clinging helplessly onto the rope that holds him to me. I swallow a laugh before it can escape. I don’t want to mock him, but the sight is kind of amusing.

I make it to Adva, flying closely next to her. “Jerk.” I bark. 

“You were taking too long,” She says, rolling her eyes with a half–smile. “I had to go or we would’ve been caught in the war all over again.” 

I roll my eyes. As we fly, we pierce the clouds, and Tulia’s blue sky slowly loses its color as we exit the atmosphere as well. It only takes a few minutes for us to fully exit the atmosphere, and looking down shows Tulia in all
of its beauty. From up here, you’d almost forget about the war. 

Up here, the sun is blinding. Our suits were crafted to duel against this as well, and automatically puts a shade over the visor to lower incoming light. Then, the suit speaks. A robotic female voice rises, and calmly says
“Journey to Earth. Approximate travel time: 89 days. Likelihood of arrival: 100%. No issues found.” 

Cheers erupt from the other fleeing elementals. Although I don’t understand… I get it’s wartorn and terrible, but why be so happy to leave your home? We’re going somewhere unknown. Few elementals have visited the planet,
and even fewer have returned. Now that I think about it, I’m scared of Gaea. What if they just capture us when we arrive and farm us like animals?

Vael breaks our bond as he can now fly himself. “I told you not to fly too fast…” he comments, his voice trembling slightly through our intercom.

“Blame Adva!” I growl. “She was going to leave us.”

Adva laughs, joining our voice channel. “You were taking too long. Look, we’re in space now. If it weren’t for me we’d still be looking up at the sky wishing we were.”

I try not to, but I can’t help but giggle. Adva’s sass is always a bit funny to me. Vael sighs, quickly changing the subject. “89 days…”

“89 days.” I responded. “Pretty generous, considering the distance…”

“Get comfortable,” Adva flipped over, flying upside down relative to us. “89 days doesn’t sound like a lot now, but it builds. Just be glad our suits have autopilot.”

I look towards the Sun. She’s right… and I still feel uneasy. I take a deep breath, uncertain but ready. Whatever comes, I must withstand. For my parents.
