— — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — CHAPTER 2 — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —
The sun shines over the edges of Gaea. Tulia has since long fallen out of view. Our escort turned back when they felt we were far enough to be safe. After that, smaller groups formed, sharing supplies and horror
stories about experiences. Save for a few unexpected deaths from suit malfunctions and lost elementals flying off-track, the trip was uneventful. 

Vael and Adva sleep peacefully, their suits on autopilot following mine. As I glide, I adore the beautiful sights of space. It’s dark and empty, but the glimmering stars in the distance kept me company when
Adva and Vael slept. I couldn’t sleep much. I was always worried I'd fly off course and get lost in space forever.

Gazing onto Gaea in person for the first time felt ethereal. I’ve only ever seen pictures in my university when they taught us about our solar system. Pictures do this beautiful planet no justice. It almost 
reminds me of Tulia. Almost.

We pass by Gaea’s only moon. Its gorgeous grayish color highlighted by the sun gives the oceans of Gaea a distinct glimmer. Lights from the planet’s sprawling cities compliment the beautiful deep blue tracing
the coasts. Its beauty is almost blinding. 

“Arrival to Earth: Approximately 6 minutes. Preparing for entry.” The suit grows, allowing  my flames to flourish for the first time in the entire trip. I take a deep breath, savoring the space gifted to me. 

The sun faded as we closed in on the dark side of Gaea. The stars I admired disappeared from the sky as the planet’s atmosphere welcomed us. 

“... We’re here already?” Called Vael from behind me. His voice was robotic and droopy as he dragged his words. 

“Already?” I flip to look back at him. Adva’s still sound asleep, so I lower my voice. I don’t know if she’s still on our voice channel.  “It felt like an eternity for me.”

“I slept through most of it.” Vael whispered. He went to rub his eyes but the visor of his helmet got in the way. His head pivoted to the others. “Looks like they’re having fun.”

I followed his gaze. The others dance as their elements flourish through the blueish sky. Flames fly, water swirls, and even sand spreads across all groups. My visor’s hit by a leaf that flew in my direction, and
I swiftly swiped it off. 

As we fly further into the atmosphere, sounds of yelling from the others roar through the air. Adva shakes awake suddenly, looking around with frantic and panicked eyes. Vael reached out to her, only to get his
hand swatted away by her.

“Relax, Adva!” I yell. “We’re safe. There’s no danger.” I maneuver closer to her, extending my hands in an attempt to comfort her. Her head frantically snaps around before she focuses on my palms. She blinks a
few times before her eyes narrow and her head tilts.

Vael giggles. “How many times has this happened?”

“Too many,” I say. “We used to wander in a Coven-Heavy area of Wrenao. Most nights were sleepless…” 

His smile fades. “Oh. I’m sorry.” His voice softens and he turns to Adva apologetically. 

Adva’s eyes slowly fixate on Vael. Her head turns slowly before she utters “Sorry… yeah.” Vael glances at me, one eyebrow raised. I simply shrug. She takes a few deep breaths off to the side, meditating as she
glides.

Once we pierce the clouds, stronger city lights blind us from the ground. Gaea’s cities are far larger than Tulia’s, each beautiful glimmer representing the sprawling human population. My imagination races with
the infinite possibilities of what these cities could possibly look like. My hair flickers at the edges, the stupid glow giving me away.

“Whatchya thinking about?” Adva asks from behind me, a curious grin on her face. She twirls in the air as she approaches.

“It’s complicated.” My gaze shifts away. Coincidentally, Vael had moved there. I stared at him for a moment before flicking my head back down to Gaea.

“Sure it is,” Adva teased. She flew below me, intercepting my gaze. “We won’t judge, right Vael?”

Vael nods after a short pause. “Uh, yeah. Totally.” When I look over at him, he simply gives me a thumbs up.

I sigh. “Just thinking about how cool the cities down there could be,” I concede. “I’m excited to explore them.” 

Vael gazes down at the bright, sprawling cities. “I agree.” He says. “They do look pretty big.”

Adva shakes her head. “Too big. They look easy to get lost in. Humans clearly have no respect for the nature of their planet, either…” 

“Well… maybe the places with no lights are where they left it?” I point at patches of green and orange. 

“Too little.” Adva scoffs. “They take more from the planet than they gave. Selfish.”

Vael and I lock eyes for a moment. Adva’s never easy to convince, so I let her believe her narrative. I think the cities look beautiful. I do think the humans could have left more for Gaea to enjoy, but at least
they left something. Who knows, they could have more people to house than Tulia.

We finally closed in on our landing spot, a snowy spot outside of a city. The center of the group lands first, and the outer rings close in to begin their landing. Adva, Vael, and I land on the cool, snow-covered
ground of Gaea. The roaring celebrations fill the sky as Elementals of all kind savor their new found freedom. 

The festivities reach us. Suddenly Adva gives me a big hug, Vael plays in the snow, and even Adva’s Windsi dances in swirls. For a moment, I feel weightless. I fly into the air, doing tricks my mother used to do
for me. Flourishing my element through the sky. Hugging and praying with strangers I've never met before. Sharing stories with people from different nations. It all feels so surreal.

Once the celebrations die down, I find myself laid in the snow next to Vael. Adva’s gone off to get someone to build us a place to stay. The camp is now mostly silent as people prepare to sleep through the cold
snowy night. 

I hear Vael sigh next to me. I stared up at the stars that accompanied us on our journey. They were still so beautiful, even down here. Staring at them reminds me that no matter how far Tulia may seem, something
is always further. I feel at ease as my chest warms. 

“How was life?” Vael asks, turning to me. “In New Iris, I mean. Before Irision.”

A smile finds its way across my face. “The best,” I say. “Nothing could beat it.” 

“Get into detail,” He turns over onto his side, facing me completely. “Explain it to me like I’m a monk from Calen.”

I giggle. “Are you a monk from Calen?” 

“No,” He smiled at my response. “But I’m a random student from Calen.”

“Imagine living in a place where you could do anything.” I begin, drawing my story in the stars. “Where all kinds of people came together to push the future forward. With schools you could learn anything you ever
wanted in record times, all while enjoying weekly arena battles broadcasted for the entire nation.”

Vael nods as I speak. “That does sound pretty nice, I guess.” He said jokingly. “Calen wasn’t much like that. I mean, it was my favorite place in the world. Everyone always tried their hardest to be as nice and as
friendly as possible.”

“I heard. I was surprised when Calen fell…”

“Blame Icilen.” I could hear the malice in his voice. “Their collapse influenced us to follow.”

“I don’t think it’s good to shift blame, Vael.” I say softly, turning my head to him slightly. “It couldn’t have entirely been them.”

“They betrayed us.” He sat up, seeming confused that I didn’t agree with him. “Betrayed our brotherhood for some superiority complex over others.”

“Vael.” I sit up, too. “The Icik are just as upset as you are. Don’t take it out on them, take it out on those who took Calen from you.”

Vael stared at me. His element flourished as I watched thoughts race through his mind. His eyes narrowed and his jaw tightened as the snow around us began to jump around, reacting to his emotion. His gaze fixates
on me, as if he was searching for a reason to agree, or disagree, while hating both options.

My chest tenses. He wouldn’t attack me, right?...  “Vael..?” I whisper, sticking my hand up slowly between us.

He finally exhales. A long, shaky, regretful sigh. “Forget it.” He mutters. “It’s nothing…” He laid back down in the snow, staring back at the sky. 

Not wanting to cause tension, I leave him be. I can tell something’s bothering him, but I don’t want to force it out. I lay next to him again, playing with a bit of the snow in between my fingers. “Whenever 
you’re ready,” I whisper. “I’ll listen.”

He flicks his eyes to me, but only for a moment. “Thanks.” he said silently, a soft cough coming after it. I couldn’t tell if he meant it or not.

The snow around us did lighten the mood. Clouds began to move in as snow began falling from the sky, slowly. 

Not another word was spoken until Adva arrived. “I got our tent!~” She had a massive smile on her face. “Now we don’t have to sleep in the freezing cold.” 

I sit up. “Thanks, Adva. Let’s find a spot—”

Commotion came from a side of the camp opposite us. Some flying creatures in the sky beamed down on us with a light too foggy to be an elemental, letting out a terrifying buzzing sound as it hovered. I jumped up to
my feet and looked as people from the other side of the camp ran towards us, fleeing from something. A few too many bump into me and I fall backwards into the snow.

The running stops. The group begins to retreat in on each other. Foggy lights from in front blinds me. Voices in an unfamiliar language approach too fast. I stand to my feet and retreat with Adva and Vael. I can hear
elements behind me flourish as I realize we are surrounded by these creatures. 

I still can’t see what these things are. I shield my eyes from the radiance in the distance by turning around to face Adva and Vael. However the voices have stopped approaching, instead they yell at us from a distance
in their unknown language.

Familiar languages roar through the skies in return as Elementals of all kinds yell back. It’s only then I realize that these creatures must be humans. They speak, yell, even, in what sounds like a comprehensive
language out of my reach. No wild animal on Tulia can do that, why would they on Gaea? 

“They’re humans,” I say. Adva and Vael look over at me. “They’re attacking us with these foggy lights.”

Vael’s gaze flicks towards the light for a moment. He turns around with Adva and I before staring at the snow, frozen. His body stiffens, hands to his side and neck slightly knelt. His expression doesn’t change, but 
a switch was flipped. I felt the air grow frigid. 

Us three have landed on the edge of the Elementals, allowing these beams to blind us unrelentingly. Adva’s jaw visibly tightens as she balls her fist. The air around us jerks randomly, throwing snow from the ground
into the air. Her fists ball, a storm brewing beneath her feet.

Both of their messages are clear. We didn’t come this far to be shut down by humans. I put my hands together, calling upon the little power I have access to. My body heats up, the snow around my feet melting, as my 
hands reach a brightness higher than the lights they use to blind us. The flames burn between my palms form a ball, a self-defense trick I learned from my father. 

Just as I prepare to hurl the attack towards the humans, a booming voice comes from them. Unlike the others, it speaks Firen with a heavy accent. “Creatures!” It begins. “Be still, we mean no harm. Please, let us
converse!”

Chants rise from the other elementals. “Lights off! Lights off!” They yell. I look down at the ball of fire, violently dancing in my hands. Suddenly the lights flick off one by one, including the ones from the sky. 
For the first time in a while, there’s no voices. In fact, the only noise comes from howling wind and the whirring animals in the sky. Everything feels still for a moment. 

The voice speaks again. “You have landed in the great Republic of Quebec. We mean no harm. We only wish to understand your goals.” 

I hear murmurs as everyone considers their answer. I look between Adva and Vael. I know I want to lay down and pass out for a few hours. 

“Food!” calls an elemental. “Freedom!” Calls another. Soon, all of their requests overlap. Sleep, quiet, answers, warmth, protection, homes, some demands get lost in the sea of others. I hear Adva yell something
behind me, but I can’t make out what she said. The silhouette of the humans moving beyond the other side of the falling snow. 

“We will allow you to stay here for some time.” The voice began again suddenly. “We can’t meet all of your demands at the moment, but we will make progress.”

Once again, the air fills with elemental festivity. Despite all that, I can’t help but consider an alternate future. What if they had tried to attack us? What if we landed somewhere more hostile, and they tried to
shoot us out of the sky? What if the moment we landed we had to fight for our freedom? 

Vael lays a hand on my shoulder. “Calm down.” He whispers. 

I scoff. “As if you weren’t preparing for a fight.” I roll my eyes and cross my arms.

“Well there’s not gonna be a fight anymore.” Adva butts in, mimicking my body language. “So, calm down!”

I sigh. “Can we please just set up our tent? I’m exhausted.”

Adva searches around as Vael and I follow close behind. We eventually find a spot under a tree, where not as much snow falls and it feels slightly warmer. “Perfect!” She says. Although she’s not facing me, I can
hear the smile on her face. She sets up the tent almost instantly, as if she did it habitually. Even she looked surprised at the haste she put it up.

“You’ve done this too much.” Vael jokes, holding back a laugh as he speaks. 

“W-Well!” She stuttered. “Maybe I moved around too much! Is that such a crime?” Her cheeks grew white as her voice cracked, the Airren flush she couldn’t hide.

Vael’s laugh inevitably escaped at the sight of an embarrassed Adva. “Oh! Adva can blush? Interesting~” He continues, teasing her. His laughter is contagious. I try to bite mine back, but it inevitably slips out.

I feel her windsi tuck itself behind me, shy and fluttery, as Adva spins around. Her voice goes low as she stares at the ground. “It’s — Whatever…”

“I think we owe Adva a thanks, Ember. Don’t you?” Vael nudges my shoulder.

I giggle. “I think so. Thank you, Adva~”

“Thank youuu Adva!” Vael’s strained attempts to contain his own laughter only makes me laugh more. 

“Can we just go to sleep?...” Adva called, still not turning back to us. 

Suddenly, an elemental from a neighbouring tent climbed out. He was Naturine, tall, and quite bulky. He approached, with each step booming through the air. 

“Quiet. Down.” He demands. 

“Woah buddy—”

He instantly cut Vael off. “Hush.” His head whipped over to Vael.  “Quiet down. Knightsdale children.”

Adva’s eyebrows furrow as the flush drains from her face. “Hey! Watch your tone, man!”

“I’m not going to ask again.” The man turned back around, stomping back to his tent, mumbling as he left.

Vael and Adva locked eyes for a short moment. Adva smirked before they both looked at me. I slowly shook my head, not wanting to be in the trouble they were planning. Before I could speak, Vael lunged a snowball 
at the back of the man’s head. 

As he slowly turned around, Vael and Adva began giggling. Vines began to grow up his arm, swarming them as he balled his fists, growling with rage. Grass and trees nearby sway despite the lack of wind. I instinctively
ball my fists, preparing for the worst. Adva and Vael, despite seeing the danger, still mock him…

“You filthy children know no manners.” The man said through gritted teeth. A vine shot out from the many growing on his arm, wrapping around Vael’s neck and lifting him off the ground. Vael kicked wildly, trying to
tear the vine off. Adva and I sprung into action. She hit him with large gusts of wind, causing snow on the ground to fly with her breeze, as I grabbed onto the vine in an attempt to burn it off. Why did they have to
do this?

A Naturine woman approached. She ran behind the man and slapped him on the back of his head. “What are you doing?! Attacking young?!” Her voice was full of rage. 

The man quickly relaxed his element, dropping Vael. “I- They attacked me first!” Adva started giggling again as Vael held his neck, coughing. 

“You don’t attack the youth!” She landed another slap on his cheek. I extended my arm to Vael, attempting to comfort him. 

“But—”

The woman cut him off. “No buts! We don’t. Attack. Youth! Do you understand?!”

The man sighed, giving a last nasty glance to us before walking off back to the tent. The woman rushed over to Vael, patting his head. “I’m sorry. Are you well?” He nods. 

Adva’s gaze locks onto me for a moment, a playful smile on her face. I shake my head at her, eyes narrowed. All of this could’ve been avoided if they just let him walk away. No, instead they chose conflict, and now
Vael’s hurt. I bite my tongue, keeping these thoughts to myself. The last thing I want to do is argue with them.

“I am so sorry,” the lady says again. “I swear, we don’t usually disrespect youth like this. Is there anything I can do for you to make it up?” 

Adva slowly approaches. “... We could use a bigger tent,” she says, softening her voice. “There’s three of us… that tent isn’t big enough.”

“I’ll make it bigger!” A smile grew across her face. “I’ll even make you some beds. I took architecture, this is my specialty!” She stood, full of enthusiasm as she rushed over to expand our tent. 

“Are you okay, Vael?” I ask, voice low. 

He’s still holding his neck. “I’m fine,” he whispers. “It didn’t even hurt, really.”

“Sure it didn’t.” Adva said, smirking. “You’re just holding your neck for fun.” Vael growled.

“No more fighting, please.” I looked at both of them. “He could’ve gotten seriously hurt.”

Adva nods. “Alright, but I’m only doing it to save you from a heart attack.” Vael said, smiling. He lets go of his neck after giving Adva a glance. 

“That’s good enough… thank you.” I responded. I feel a weight lifted off my shoulders. I want them both to be safe, but it feels like they just want the opposite. 

Adva comes, sitting in front of Vael in the snow. I look over at the Naturine woman to admire her work. She was extending our tent into a much larger one, extending from the tree it was placed under. It’s not the
largest, but it’s enough to fit us 3, and that’s all that matters. 

Watching her makes my flames quiet, curling inward instead of flaring out. She grows vines and extends leaves on the trees to cover any holes in the tightly woven flowers that make up the tents walls. When she 
notices me watching, she smiles, and makes a flower the color of my flames, a bright orange. 

When I look away, I notice Vael and Adva staring at me. “What? Is there something on my face?” I ask, reaching up to touch my lips.

Adva shakes her head. “Vael was staring at you first, so I just joined in.” A smile creeps across her face, which she quickly covered with her hand. 

“I- was just admiring you, I guess.” Vael says softly, looking away.

I feel the warmth bloom in my stomach. I pause, expecting him to explain further. “Admiring me?” I tilt my head, buying myself time to collect my thoughts.

“Yeah, I…” he hesitates. “... never really saw any Firen people in person. Your flames are… cool.”

“Oh.” I mutter. The words settle in my mind. Heat rises in the back of my neck, rushing to my face. “Thanks…” I whisper, barely.

Adva chuckles. “How cute.”

Vael laughs, almost too loud, rubbing the back of his neck. “That came out more serious than I was expecting!” He shifts, eyes shifting everywhere but me. He clears his throat. “I should probably stop talking now.”

Adva’s laughter fills the air. I keep my flames tucked in, refusing to let the embers betray me.

The lady approaches. “It’s finished, ready for you to rest!” She smiles from ear-to-ear. “Again, I apologize for how he acted. He’s… ill.”

“It’s alright ma’am,” Adva says, speaking for Vael and I. She bows her head slightly. “We thank you for doing this.”

“You know your manners, nascent.” She pats Adva’s head. “But no need, I am just happy to help. Sleep well.” She walks off back to her tent, with a small wave. 

Vael jumps to his feet, quickly entering the tent without any words. Adva slowly approaches me. “If you ask me, you’d make a cute couple.”

The blush I had been fighting to hide floods my face. Adva’s words delivered the finishing blow as all the flames and flares I suppressed burst out at once in a tiny explosion, melting the snow around me. 

Adva chuckles. “That struck a nerve. Come on, let’s get you to bed.” She extends her hand to me. “You said you were tired, right?”

I take her hand, trying to force what she said out of my mind. I don’t know if I’m ready for that kind of commitment. I dart my eyes to every little detail we pass, trying to think about it as little as possible.
Everything, from the discolored tree bark to the elementals setting up tents to the twin snowflakes falling to the ground distracts me. 

The inside of the tent is far more gorgeous than I could’ve expected. Bioluminescent flowers hang from the top creating a small night light. The aroma stings my nose immediately, the soft smell causing my ears to perk.
My flames burn inward again, suppressing themselves within the welcoming environment. 

The beds are packed tightly in the tent, directly next to each other. Vael sleeps on the one furthest to the right, leaving the other two for Adva and I.

I lay down in the middle, right next to Vael. He snores so softly, it’s almost soothing. I heard Adva lay behind me. “Please, get some sleep, Ember.” She whispered. “You need it… seriously.” I don’t respond.

I hear her shift. The biolumenescent flowers dim their lights, and the outside quiets. My eyes shut slowly, as if waiting for me to fight it. But for once, I feel my mind slow down and my heart relax. My flames 
quiet and dim as everything goes black. I’ve forgotten the peace of true sleep, but I hope to remember it tonight.
