I clutched my father’s book close to my chest, sitting on my new bed. The sounds of my own flames burning only increased my anxiety. This could very well be one of the last times I enjoy their sounds, before 
Gaea forces me to hide them away. The darkness in the room, only pushed away by my own light, could engulf me as it does everyone else. I never considered the possibility of me being afraid of the dark… but 
right now, it feels almost suffocating. 

I hear a soft knock on the door before it creaks open. In peeks Amei’s head, who quickly flicks on my light. “Are you awake?” She asked softly. I nodded, wondering how she didn’t come to that conclusion herself
after seeing me sitting up in my bed, eyes wide open. “I’ve brought you some clothes.” 

She fully enters the room, holding a large low-hanging bag, sealed off at the top. “They’re human clothes made for Elementals.” She places the bag on my bed at my feet. “I think you’ll love them.”

I don’t respond, but I lean forward to display interest. Though, I’m worried these clothes only serve to suffocate my flames and make me appear more human. 

She opens the bag, pulling out an outfit— a black cropped shirt, highlighted with orange at the sleeves, with lines leading up to the neck, looped in the same orange color. Just looking at it, I can tell it 
would fit me perfectly, as if it were made to fit my proportions. 

Then, right after it, she pulls out black, baggy pants, both legs having more pockets than I could ever need. It has the same color of orange, this time riding up the sides of the legs in stripes. They look 
like they’d be tight around the waist. 

Amei lays the outfit on my lap. “... The bag only has about 5-6 more of the same outfit.” She smiles awkwardly. “Sorry. They don’t really make much else for you.”

“... It’s alright,” I say softly. “Haven’t had a new outfit in years. Can’t be too upset.”

She stands up. “I’ll leave now… please try on the clothes and let me know if something’s wrong.” I nod, and she walks out, leaving my light on. 

I sigh, going to close and lock the door. I consider turning out the lights again, but I decide against it. I want good lighting to see myself in my mirror when I put on this outfit. I approach my bed again,
taking off my shirt. There’s already a decently sized cut in it by the neck, and it’s pretty dirty, its red color being stained by dirt. 

Then I slip out of my pants, which have holes of their own by my knees and ankles. Now that I’m looking at them in my hands, it feels kind of sad. They’ve been terrorized by my desperate movements for years 
at this point. I feel odd abandoning them now, but I must move forward.

I enter the bathroom connected to my bedroom, flicking on the light so I can see. Amei informed me that Gaea’s showers are very similar to Tulia, but warned me that the interfaces may be confusing. How confusing
could a shower really be? 

A sliding glass door separated me from the shower’s area. Easy enough, just slide it open. There were towels and soaps ready and waiting for me on a few metal grates bolted to the brick wall. 

Every interface has been easy… up until this point. Trying to figure out how to turn the shower on felt like trying to slay a dragon without my element. On Tulia, there’s a button to press, then a holographic 
interface appears for temperature. On Gaea, however, there’s no button. I’m stuck with this odd-looking lever-ish handle, and yet pulling it only gives me resistance. 

Feeling defeated, I go to bring the new outfit into the bathroom with me, taking the time to think of other ways to interact with the lever. Suddenly, it hit me: I never tried turning it. I pick up the new outfit,
take it with me to the bathroom and lay them on the sink. Then, I return to the handle to turn it.

Cold water rushes out, sending shivers throughout my body as it rushes to freeze me. I hold back a scream and quickly jump away, now cold and helpless. It’s on, but it’s the wrong temperature. I look from the
outside for any kind of holographic screen, finding nothing. I try to reach for the lever again, but I can’t get to it without water hitting me. 

I consider asking for help, but rule it out quickly. Asking for help with a shower? What am I, a child? I can do this. I reach in, the cold biting my skin, forcing me to clench my own jaw. My entire body
tenses as I grab the lever, pushing it as far as I can.

Finally, the water warms to a glorious heat. I turn the lever until I can’t anymore, yet the water still isn’t as hot as I’d like it to be. Oh well, it’s hot enough to clean myself, I declare in my thoughts.
I step into the shower, the now hot water purging the feeling of the cold water from a few moments earlier. 

The water hitting my skin dampens my flames slightly. The towels I use are singed against my skin. The soap is incredibly slippery, making it hard to hold onto. I sigh, showers are much simpler on Tulia. My
flames even evaporate the water regularly, causing steam to hit me instead of the desired water. 

After a half an hour showering, I step out, raising my temperature to dry myself. The soap I was given was incredibly annoying to handle, but it admittedly smells amazing. It makes me imagine a field of 
flowers, accompanied by the beautiful wildlife that inhabits it. I wonder where they got this stuff.

I slip into my new outfit, admiring myself in the mirror. The sleeves are kind of short and the pants slightly baggier than I’d like, but other than that, I oddly love it. The way it interacts with my flames,
allowing them to flow under the baggage of the pants, and move out of the fabric clinging to my torso. To top it all off, the orange matches me perfectly.

I step out of the bathroom, a new found confidence following me. New, fun clothes will always get my mood up. To go with it, I get a necklace that my mother made for me from my bag. It’s decorated with
multiple rare Tulian gems, all of which shine in the darkness. My mother called it the Beacon for that reason. I place it around my neck, the black frame and orange jewels matching the outfit. 

I emerge from my room, walking down the hallway to reach the living room, where I find Kael sitting on the couch watching the news. I wave to him lightly, but he seems fascinated by something else.

“I wasn’t told we were getting new clothes.” He said, his eyes wide. “That outfit looks great on you. Really gives Imbra, you know?”

I can’t help but smile at his compliment. “Thank you.” I whisper, turning my head towards the ground. I sit on the couch next to him. “What are you watching?”

“The news.” He answers, re-focusing on the screen. “Apparently there’s lots of war on Gaea, too.”

I tilt my head. “Really?”

He nods. “All the news has been talking about is rising tensions, terrorist attacks, and border skirmishes.” 

I look at the screen, noticing the Firen translations in subtitles spreading across the bottom half. That’s nice, I think to myself. At least they interpreted this for us. “Sounds like the news right
before Dal yon Tulia.” I whisper.

“I noticed that as well.” He glances over at me for a moment. “You think they’re on the verge of a world war?” 

“Right as we arrive, right?” I sigh. “Only then their world goes to shit.”

He laughs. “Wouldn’t be surprised.” 

Adva then walks in from the hallway, wearing a new outfit as well. She rocks a black jacket, white angular seam lines cutting across her chest. The white extends down the top half of the oversized sleeves,
returning to black at her elbows and clenching her wrists. Below she wears an asymmetrical skirt, hovering right above her knees. White highlights loop around the bottom of the skirt, rising up the sides,
meeting with highlights on her jacket by her waist. On her jacket rests a white harness, as if waiting patiently for a weapon to hold.

“Adva!!” I stand quickly. Yrusei seems excited about the outfit too, swirling and rushing all across the open room. “You look wonderful!” 

She smiles. “I know I do~ but you look better. Twirl for me, let me get a good look at the outfit!”

Fulfilling her request, I playfully spin around slowly, allowing her to fully view my outfit. I hear her giggling as she takes it all in. “Very you! Very cute!” She turns to Kael. “You better make sure the 
other men stay away.”

I gasp. “Adva! Stop!” She just keeps giggling. Kael facepalms with both hands, shaking his head. 

“You were already good looking. With this outfit? You’re too much.” She pokes my shoulder, a smug smile on her face. 

I can’t help but smile. “Okay, Adva, I get it!” 

“I’m so serious!” She adjusts my collar, pulling my sleeves higher. “If I were a man, I’d—”

“Okay!!” I yell, cutting her off. “That’s enough! For real this time!” 

She just giggles, her hand brushing over my hair. “You smell nice, too. How long did it take you to figure out the shower?”

“Longer than I’d like to admit,” I answer, a smile creeping on my face. “And the water kept evaporating.” 

“Oh, that’s terrible.” She looks over to Kael. “How about you? Was the shower challenging?” 

“I haven’t taken one yet,” He says through a sigh. “Amei told me to wait for my new clothes first.”


I tilt my head. “Well be ready for a challenge.” Adva comments, her tone rising as she speaks. “And some sub-par water.” Kael only sighs. 

As if we summoned her, Amei walks through the front door, a similar bag she gave to me hanging on her shoulder. “Come on, Vael.” She says softly. She still thinks his name is Vael… why hasn’t he corrected he
r yet? “I’ve  got your clothes here.” 

Amei and Kael walk off down the hallway, his hand brushing mine as he walks by. Adva rushes, jumping onto the couch where he last sat. “What’s this all about?”

“The news,” I respond. “Kael was watching it. It was talking about war, apparently.” 

“Well, uh, not anymore.” Adva shifts, her shoulders dipping as her eyebrows furrow. “They’re talking about us now.” 

What? I walk forward to bring the screen into my view, and sure enough- the refugee camp we left is on it. We’re on it, too, entering the vehicle that Amei had us leave in, but the center of focus seems to be
the camp. I watch as the subtitles flash words like “fear”, “debate”, “safety”, and “conflict” before they are swiftly censored and changed to other, unrelated words. 

The camera fades to a man standing behind a podium, speaking about Elementals as if we were lesser. He only speaks for a few seconds, words like “contain” and “study” flashing, before the broadcast freezes.
Then, it cuts to a new channel— now showing animated characters attempting to slay a dragon in quick-paced and high colored frames. 

Adva looks over at me. “We saw the same thing, right?” 

I hear my heart beating in my chest as I nod. I don’t even know what to think. I already concluded that Gaea was hostile, but the depth of such I was still unfamiliar with. Those words they used were degrading
and intentional. 

Adva shakes her head. “Let them try,” She whispers. “We survived a war on Tulia. An attack on Gaea will hardly compare.”

“Adva,” I say, shaking my head. “We’re in unfamiliar territory. Not only that, we don’t know what they’re capable of.”

“I’d wager that their capabilities aren’t even half ours,” Adva says, snickering. “There’s a reason they need to ‘contain’ us, right?”

I trip over my rebuttals, striking each down with my own extensive thought. 

Adva nods. “You know I’m right.” 

I sigh. “The last thing we need right now is conflict, Adva. Please.” 

“They’re the ones asking for it.” She points to the screen with her thumb, which still displays the fast paced action cartoon. “I’m just defending myself.”

“Well what if all humans aren’t inherently evil, like you think?” I ask loudly, my eyebrows furrowing. “Amei’s a good example. She—”

“She’s a part of the system built to contain us.” Adva sits up, her head tilting forward. “Even if her intentions are well, her purpose is not.”

I pause, shaking my head. “I feel like you just want the world to be hostile.” I say softly. 

“It is hostile.” She tilts her head. “Have you not realized?”

“... I have.” I break our eye contact, looking towards the ground. “I’m still harboring hope that things may change.”

She walks to me, standing directly in front of me. When I look up at her, she places a hand on my shoulder, a soft smile rising. “Gaea isn’t home, anyway.” Her voice is soft, Yrusei hugging my leg. “You 
have us. We survived a hostile world before, we can do it again.”

I nod in agreement. “But what about the ambiguity of human ability?”

“Wrenao was unfamiliar when you arrived, right?” I nod. “And you survived it. Put some faith in yourself, Imbra.” 

But that was Tulia. That was a different world with different systems and rules. Gaea and Tulia are sister planets in name, but nothing alike in practice. The same ways I survived Wrenao will not apply
here.

“Love ya.” Adva says with a smile before returning to her seat. Yrusei stays by my side, now climbing my pants and resting on my waist. I go to cradle her, but it’s hard due to her invisibility. She climbs 
up my arm, and I feel her nibble on my hair, tugging it as she does. 

“Hey, no—” I whisper. Her tugging causes a stinging sensation on my scalp. Suddenly, she stops. She goes completely still, her light breeze stopping with her.

Then, Xor enters through the front door, wearing a backpack. His head snaps to me. “Ah, Imbra.” A practiced smile appears on his face. “Just who I was looking for. Come with me, please.” 

I look over to Adva, who’s gaze has shifted to the ground, trying as hard as she can to not look at Xor. “Okay.” I mumble. Yrusei jumps off of my arm, off towards Adva. Xor walks out a door leading to the 
backyard, and I follow behind him. 

The backyard is open and spacious, sunlight laying upon every inch of ground. “How’s your day been, Imbra?” Xor calls from in front of me. “I see you got your outfit in.”

I nod, forgetting he cannot see me. “Yes, it’s quite comfortable.” I answer softly. “My day has been pleasant. Why are we out here?” 

“Patience, Imbra.” He says, emptying his bag by flipping it and dumping its contents onto the ground. A bunch of Tulian Firewood falls out, laying itself upon the ground in a messy pile.

I tilt my head. “Okay… well, how’s your day been?” I ask, out of courtesy. I’m more curious as to what the firewood is for. 

He begins to talk about his day, but I hardly pay attention. I watch as he spreads the firewood into a large, wide circle, sparsely placing more inside the space. There’s no particular order or pattern to
it, which only serves to confuse me further.

“Please sit in the circle.” He says, the same, practiced smile rising on his face.

I stare at him for a moment, waiting for a reason. After a few seconds without one, I just walk to the middle and sit, crossing my legs. “This seems really invasive. I’d like to know what you’re doing.”

“We’re unlocking your val.” Xor says softly, spreading white ashes on the outer edges of the firewood circle.

I freeze, head turning away from him. Unlocking me? Why? And how does he know how to do that? Unlocking val on Tulia was illegal unless you were military personnel, and that’s one of the only laws to hold
strong even during Dal yon Tulia. Maybe he was a veteran before the war. 

Still- what if I don’t want to be unlocked? He didn’t even ask me how I felt about it. Before I can speak up, he lights the circle, the flames rising around me and blocking my view outward. Despite not having 
contact, the sparsely placed firewood lights themselves one by one, slowly getting closer to me.

My heart races. I don’t want to move, messing up the process is irreversible. As the flames approach, my breathing slows and my head feels light. My own flames jump, dancing in unusual ways. My entire body
quivers, and I feel pain begin rising from my chest. 

One bright light blinds me. I close my eyes tight, but when I open them, I’m somewhere else. A plain, completely burned and singed by unrelenting flames, still burning in the distance. I’m suddenly on my
feet as well, an unfamiliar feeling rushing through my body. 

Each move I make splits the ashes left behind by the flame’s rampage. I approach where it currently burns, flux-flying through the wreckage. My entire body still shakes uncontrollably, but it's manageable.
I ignore it for now. 

The flames burn louder than they should. Their embers land further away than possible. They dance faster than usual. When I reach to touch them, it burns me, a sensation I never thought I’d actually feel.

When I turn back from where I came, the environment is different— now the flames surround me, burning trees and plants not there previously. I feel as the air rejects me, stealing my breaths. I fall to my
knees, the lack of oxygen rendering me powerless.

Then, a black humanoid figure emerges. Covered in ash and cut unevenly, the flames clear a path for it. It floats towards me, void of any defining features, before landing directly in front of my hands. 
“Imbra Hayes.” it says. As it speaks, I feel my flames retreat. 

I can hardly turn my head up to face it. It kneels before me, the sheer heat of it nearly burning me. “Do not surrender.” It whispers. 

When it utters that, I feel strength rush through my body, though still lacking oxygen. My hands swim through the ash on the ground, pushing myself back up to my feet. I hear it chuckle. 

“Strong creation.” It says. I feel its eyes piercing my flesh. “Yes, indeed. She was right.”

I try to speak, but I can’t. I keep panting, searching desperately for air. Suddenly, the figure extends its hand, a weapon resting within. A small, yet sharp dart, shaped by flames and ash. It stares at
me expectantly, its eyes meeting mine causing a shiver down my spine.

When I grab the dart, the contact on the figure’s skin singes my hand. But I feel as my body accepts the weapon, it joins my flames as my own.

“Do you feel it yet, Hayes?” The voice boomed, my own flames struggling to cover me. “You do.” 

“F… feel what?” I asked, my voice small as I hardly managed to utter the question. 

The figure smiled, still without a mouth. It didn’t answer me, but it observed my struggles. Too many questions ran through my mind— what is this thing? Where am I? Am I dead? Did I mess it up?

The figure shook its head. “Thinking too much.” It said softly, turning away from me. “Focus, or you will be dead very soon.” 

I felt my body struggling without air. I fell back to my knees, gasping for even the slightest bit of oxygen. The surrounding fires rose as my vision blurred. 

“Not as strong as I thought.” The figure mocked. “Focus, Imbra.”

My life begins to flash before my eyes. Not the war. Not starvation. The voices that comforted me. The gifts that made me smile. The mistakes I never got to correct. I can’t leave now. There’s still so
much I have to live for. 

Using what little strength I have left, I push off the ground. My body quivers as my flames begin to retreat, a fiery sweat replacing them. I plant one foot, then a few seconds later, the other, then stand
up straight, still gasping for air. I stumble back and forth, but hold my balance eventually, staring at the figure.

It claps. “You still cannot breathe.” 

As if I didn’t know that. I growl, fighting my entire body’s instinct to collapse. I close my eyes, trying to focus on breathing. Each gasp brings in just enough air to keep me standing, relieving my stress.
I ball my fists, my flames rising again, a new energy accompanying them.

The air turns. The ashes jump. Flames around me retreat. The air accepts me, allowing me to breathe once again. My growls are now louder than the surrounding flames, as the flames on me flicker between red
and black, and their usual color. 

“There it is.” The figure says. “If only you knew what you caused.”

Before I can ask, I’m suddenly right back where I was before, in the middle of the firewood circle. I quickly stand to my feet, the wall of fire absent. I feel a new energy coursing through my veins. My 
flames burn differently, and my heart beats harder than before.

Xor walks over calmly, a different smile rising on his face. “How do you feel?”

My body feels hollow. I look down at my hands, the dart now gone, but the singe from the figure is still there. I look back up at Xor, before collapsing, losing consciousness. 

