“YRUSEI!” Adva yells loudly. Repeatedly. I could hear wind whistling throughout the house— but it was too strong to be Yrusei. The urgency in Adva’s voice causes me to rush out to investigate.

She is completely tearing the house apart. The couch was flipped on its side, doors open, shelves with their contents emptied on the floor, all while Xor and Amei stood in the doorway to the backyard, Amei looking
quite worried, observing Adva’s chaos. 

When she notices me, she dashes to me, faster than I have ever seen her move. Her hands land on my shoulders, tears pouring down her face. “Please tell me you’ve seen— felt, Yrusei. Please.” Her voice was soft,
and slow.

I shake my head. “I- I just got up, Adva. I don’t—” 

She collapses to her knees in front of me. Her soft weeping pierces my soul, causing tears to well in my eyes, as well. I kneel down, wrapping my arms around her. 

“... She’s got to be here somewhere.” Amei said softly, moving closer to us. She lays a hand upon Adva’s back. “Is there any way I can help?” 

“Yrusei never strays far.” Adva says, her voice cracking as she speaks. “I- She’s always in my vision.” 

Amei looks up at me. “Please breathe, Adva.” I say softly, my voice shaky. “We’ll find her.”

“We shouldn’t have to find her.” Adva looks up at me. A tear rolls down her cheek. “She’s always right there. So easy to find.” 

Before I can say anything else, she jumps into my arms, wrapping hers around me. The wind coming from her stops completely, falling motionless. Just like her wind, she also freezes, stiff and rigid. Her breathing 
grows shallow as I wrap my arms around her. 

I can hear her voice refusing to speak by my ear, stutters and half-words coming out in between her cries. Everything in the room feels unnervingly still and silent. I rub her back, trying anything to slow her
dread.

Xor walks in all the way finally, calm and slow. He approaches us, leaning against the wall. Amei and I look at him. 

“Adva,” he says softly, the same practiced smile on his face. She turns over to him. “When you unlock your val, your windsi disappears.” 

I feel Adva jerk. She turns to him quickly, face drowning in emotions I can’t translate. “Y-You-...” She hardly manages, her entire body quivering. 

Xor puts his hands up in front of his chest. “It had to happen eventually, Adva. I simply sped up the process.”

Adva began growling. Then, her growl turned to a rage-filled scream. Winds pulsated from her body, nearly knocking me on my feet. Wallpaper was torn off the wall by the sheer force of her rage. In the blink
of an eye, she had tackled Xor, bringing him to the ground, as her tornado of rage tossed objects all around the house, blowing embers of my own flame off of my skin. 

“YOU KILLED HER!” She yells repeatedly. I can’t see what’s happening, but I can hear Xor struggling to breathe, alongside impacts upon flesh. I’m knocked backwards by Adva’s winds, Amei grabbing onto my arm 
for support. 

Then suddenly, it stops. The winds stop howling. The house stops shaking. Objects stop flying. I sit up quickly, Xor pushing Adva’s unconscious body off of him to the ground before he stands up. “Unlocking 
val tends to make the elemental emotional.” he says, his voice still calm. He rolls his shoulders, his bones audibly cracking back into place. There was blood leaking from his lip and nose. 

Amei and I stand. “Are you sure it isn’t because you killed her lifelong companion?” Amei asks loudly. I can hear the offense in her voice. 

Xor merely glances at her. “She should wake up in about an hour or so.” He walks to the front door, brushing himself off. He clears his throat. “Call me when she and Kael are both awake.” 

“She’s going to kill you.” I say quickly. 

Xor chuckles. “No she won’t. Because you won’t let her.”

I recoil. Who does he think he is? “Says who?” 

“If you know what’s good for you,” He turns, his element flaring as he stares me down. “You say.” 

I feel my flames rising with rage. My fist balls and quivers, a dart automatically manifesting in my hand. Before I can act, Xor exits the house. I still throw the dart, the door exploding into flames and
ash.

Amei acts quickly, grabbing a fire extinguisher and suppressing the flames before they can spread. She turns to me. “... I’m sorry.” She says softly, approaching me. “I swear I didn’t know anything about 
this.”	

I look down at my hands. How did a dart appear? It was identical to the one I grabbed in that… world. The way it felt in my hand was almost… too perfect. It conducted my flames too well. It was too easy to
throw. Thinking about it feels uncanny.

“Hey.” Amei says softly, grabbing my attention as I look up at her. “I’ll get to the bottom of this. Okay?” I nod. She slides me a device, looking similar to a small version of her tablet. “You can message
me using this. Let me know when Kael and Adva wake up.”

“Okay.” I nod, my voice small. A small, cautious smile rises on her face, before she turns to exit. 

Adva’s still on the floor, her limp body looking uncomfortable. I approach her, opting to help instead of leaving her there. There’s a small crater in the ground where she tackled Xor… I hope she’s okay.

I pick her up, her limbs drooping downwards. Then I walk her to my room and lay her on my bed. I move her into a more comfortable position, silently hoping she won’t wake up in a panic and destroy my room. 

Exiting my room, I find Kael standing at the end of the hallway, motionless. Only his head darts around, glancing at all the chaos Adva’s rage had caused. It looked like a tornado had torn through the room,
nearly flipping everything on its head.

“Behind you.” I mumble softly as I pass him, trying not to spook him. 

He turns to me. “What happened out here?” His voice was low, as if trying not to be heard.

I stuttered and tripped over my words a bit before sighing. “Alot.” I manage, tiling the couch back onto its legs. 

Kael comes over to help me. “Too much to explain?”

“Yrusei’s gone.” I say softly. “Xor told Adva to unlock, without telling her it would cause Yrusei to disappear.”

He recoils. “What?”

I nod. “Then when I said she’d kill him, I.. think he threatened me.”

Kael’s eyebrows furrow, his head tilting downwards. “He did what?”

“Yeah.” We finally put the couch back where it belongs. I turn to him. “He flourished and said, ‘you won’t let her’.” 

Kael stares at me blankly, his gaze only being broken by his blinks. “Did you do anything?”

I point at the front door. “See how it’s singed and burned?” He looks over. “I threw a… dart thing at it. Right after he left.”

“A… dart?” He turned back to me. “Where’d you get a dart?” 

I go to pick up the television, attempting to place it back on its stand, the only thing still standing normally. “It kind of just appeared in my hand.” 

“Can I see?” He sits on the couch as I put the television where it belongs.

I turn to him. “I don’t know if I can do it on command.”

“Have you tried?” He asks, leaning forward towards me. I shake my head. “Then how can you be sure?”

I look down at my hand. Focusing on my own body causes me to notice the difference flowing through my veins. This energy feels different than anything I’ve ever felt, but reminds me of my fight for oxygen
in that world. Just the act of focusing on it causes my flames to jerk and dance in unusual ways. My light shines brighter than it ever has, and I feel my heat rise. 

Kael shields his eyes. “All that for a dart?” He says, giggling. 

I ball my fist, a large fire rising from it. It’s not a dart, but it’s different. Then I remember- My father’s book. I have read it sparsely, but mainly searching for the notes he left me. Perhaps there’s
something in there to teach me how to properly conduct this new energy. 

“I’ll be right back.” I say quickly. I hear him mumble ‘okay’ as I run off to my room. Seeing Adva on my bed, still motionless, reminds me that there’s a reason for me to do this. To protect us, all three
of us. I can’t be sidelined anymore, I have to take initiative. 

I march to my stuff, a new found confidence following me. I grab my book from its drawer, and quickly return to Kael, who sits patiently waiting. 

“Ah, your book!” He sits up, intrigued. “This is going to get interesting. Let me join.” 

I sit next to him, opening to the book’s Table of Contents. There’s nearly a hundred chapters leaning on many different topics, like mid-battle focus, stamina management, weapon usage, and the one I’m 
looking for: How to control Val. 

I open to that page of the book and begin reading, imagining the words in my head as my father’s voice speaking to me. It goes beautifully in depth, even explaining how val truly works as the biological 
agent activating our element, given to us by the Gods to make us like them, and how no matter how hard we try, we cannot artificially replicate it. 

Kael and I sat together reading the chapter for a while. I lost track of time after the first few minutes, but we eventually finished the entire thing. When I placed the book at my side, I realized how 
close we were— my shoulders pressed against his as his head leaned close to mine. 

“Did you get all that?” I say softly, my head slightly turned toward him.

He nods slowly. “Yeah.” His eyes lock onto mine, his breath rolling down my lips. “Your father was a good writer.”

“He was.” I giggle, my heart fluttering. “A good speaker, too. He knew how to make things sound simple." 

“Well… do you want to try all that?” He leans closer. I feel our heartbeats synchronize as we hold each other's hands. 

After a short pause, I nod. “... Y-Yeah. I should.” I stand up, leaving the book where I sat. Our hands linger together for a moment before slipping apart as I move to a more open area, as to not harm 
him with my flames.

He sits up, clearing his throat. “Let’s see what you got.” 

I ball my fist again, new knowledge assisting me. The energy coursing through my veins now feels less alien and more like potential. When the flame rises from my fist, I’m now commanding it. Its odd 
dancing and sharp turns are by my command, each action intentional. 

Kael nods. “Okay, the flame’s dancing. How about the dart?” He asks, leaning forward. 

Right, the dart! There wasn’t anything about weapons in the chapter we read, but I think I can figure it out. I push the energy through my body— every movement burning slightly— until there’s an excess
of it in my palm. The pure energy gathered there causes my hand to shine brighter than the rest of my body, the heat causing the air around it to distort. 

First attempt: Imagining the dart. Maybe if I convince myself it’s there, it’ll actually appear. To do this, I recall how it felt in my hands. How its fire connected with mine. How my fingers curled 
around it. I close my eyes, trying to immerse myself in the memory, only to realize it’s not working. It does little beyond shift val through my body, between my head and arm. 

I sigh. Second attempt: Finger positions. Even though it appeared on its own last time, I still try this as a ‘just in case’. I position my fingers in multiple ways that would be optimal for holding a 
dart, multiple shapes failing to satisfy. When I pinch my thumb to my index finger, however. I feel it— the same energy the dart caused when it first manifested. 

Kael tilts his head. “Getting somewhere?”

I smile at his distraction, but quickly refocus. Pinching them together harder does nothing, so I try to imagine it again. Suddenly, with just the thought of the dart in my hand, it appeared. Same shape,
same form, same feel. I looked at it, still holding it between my thumb and index, twisting my wrist around to examine its shape.

“Cool.” Kael leans forward, a large smile on his face. “Now throw it.”

“Throw it?” I recoil. “Look at the door! That’s what happened last time I threw this thing.”

He curls his lips inward, bobbing his head side to side. “Sure, but what if it does something different?” 

“I- well..” I pause, trying to think of a good response.

“Only one way to tell, right?” He stares, eyes full of excitement. 

I look around the room, searching for something that wouldn’t burn for too long or easy to contain. I eventually choose the kitchen island- it’s made of a stone from Gaea, and hard to move. I don’t 
think Gaea’s stone burns easily, but- as Kael said, only one way to find out. 

I throw the dart, the ease of which catching me off guard a second time. When it makes contact with the island, it doesn’t explode. Instead, it pierces the stone, leaving a small hole where it entered. 

“Huh.” Kael says softly. “I kind of expected worse.”

“Me… too.” I respond slowly. It's only then I notice his new outfit. He wore utility pants very similar to mine, with less pockets and slightly more baggage. On top, he wore a heavy black insulated jacket,
unzipped, absorbing light instead of reflecting it. Both the jacket and pants had white stripes going  down the sides, meeting in a circle around his waist. Under his jacket rested a basic white shirt,
nothing too special to note about it. 

He turns back to me, staring at me for a moment. “Do you want to continue reading your book? I honestly was having a lot of fun.”

I nod eagerly. “Absolutely.” I rush back next to him, picking the book up again and opening to the first chapter this time: Absolute Basics. The chapter covered things like unwritten rules, good practices, 
and harmony with your own element. It felt like my father was right beside me, whispering each word on the page into my ear.

My proximity to Kael was hard to ignore, too. Not that I wanted to, quite the opposite. His shoulder, cold and icy, pressed against mine, the temperature difference taking me back. I found it hard to focus
on the book at times, especially when his hand found its way onto my own, or when our heads touched. Both of our elements were calm, flowing through the area cautiously. 

I lost track of how many chapters we read, and how long it took. We learned plenty, from how to fight without having knowledge on combat, to myths and stories like Forbidden Elements. My father seemed
fascinated with one specifically called Hellfire, spending about 5 pages on it alone. 

Amei walks through the door, soaked by rain. She closes it behind her quickly, urgency in each movement. 

Kael and I jump, quickly turning in her direction. “What’s wrong?” I ask quickly, approaching her.

She rushes over to me. “Listen- Listen, you do not ever leave the house. Okay?” She places her hands on my shoulders.

“What? What’s happening?” I ask again- my anxiety rising. I hear Kael stand to his feet behind me.

She checks over her shoulder to the door, checking to make sure it’s closed. “Please, don’t be afraid.” She lowers her voice, becoming softer, almost motherly. “My organization is turning against you. Someone
bad took over, and he’s making radical changes.”

“What?” Kael steps closer. “Are we safe? Should we leave?”

“No, no! Under no circumstances do you leave this house!” She says quickly. “I am not leaving either. We are staying here together.”

My heart begins to race. “What does this mean for us?” I trip over my words, my heat rising.

She turns her head to me, staring me in my eyes for a moment. “Nothing good.” She finally utters. “Listen, I… know what he did. But Xor’s the only other person you know who isn’t on their side.”

I stumble over my words again, my  flames flourishing near Amei’s hands. She pulls away. “Are you asking us to trust him?” 

“No.” She shakes her head. “I’m asking you to endure him. I don’t like him that much either, but he can protect you in ways I can’t”

Kael steps forward. “Why do we need protecting?” He scoffs. “We could—”

“They will overwhelm you, Kael.” She says. “Hundreds of them versus the three of you. Please, just trust me. We have a plan to get you off the radar.” 

I feel his suspicious gaze upon her from over my shoulder. “What do we do now?”

“Stay together.” She turns her head back to the door once more. “Don’t talk to anyone you don’t know. If you do have to leave, keep your heads low.” 

“Can we please know what’s happening exactly?” Kael’s voice booms from behind me. “Beyond the ‘institution’ becoming evil.”

Amei stands up, glaring out the window. “There’s this man. His name’s Troy. Troy Talona.” She turns back to us, eyes darting around the open space. “One of the most anti-elemental people out there. He’s just
taken control of the institution that was responsible for keeping you housed, fed, and safe. Is that enough for you?”

Kael’s eyes widen as he nods slowly. “Yeah. Yeah, that’s enough.”

“Good.” She rushes around the room, closing all the blinds. 

I hear carnage coming from down the hall. Fast, whistling winds. Covers being tossed around. Adva woke up. Without a second thought, I run back into my room, barging through the door. I was lucky to make 
it in when only half of my room was destroyed. 

Adva’s head instantly snaps to me when I barge in, causing a few objects to fly at me, though small and insignificant. I immediately rush to her side, placing my hands on her arm. 

“Be calm, Adva.” I say in Airren. “You are safe.” I honestly didn’t know if I could verify that statement.

Her breathing was quick and unpredictable, just like the storm she caused in the room. Her eyes examined me closely, as if remembering who I was. As she did, the winds slowed. Objects stopped flying. Covers
stopped flowing. The room quieted. 

“Yrusei.” She uttered, her voice cracking.


