Adva just wasn’t the same. Her eyes were missing a distinct glimmer. Her hands twitched more often. She fixated on small things- sounds of ventilation, bright flashing from outside, the dripping of water in the 
sink. Every time I tried to talk to her, her gaze always seemed to stare through me. She responds, but it’s never her usual tone, something I can’t pinpoint missing from each sentence. But this morning, that
was different.

“Imbra?” She said, as she pushed her way through my door into my room. 

I looked up at her from my desk, interrupting my studies of my father’s book to see her. “Yes?”

She closed the door behind her, softly. Then, she approached, standing directly next to me. The wind around her swirled in odd ways, carrying a chilly breeze with them. Every movement she made was slightly
stiff, as if she was failing to hide her own anxiety.

“What… am I?” She asked, her voice low. I tilt my head in confusion. “To you, I mean.” Her voice shook with each word. 

I think for a moment. Though I hadn’t ever thought about the answer on my own time, it came to me easily. “You’re like the sister I never had.” I say confidently. “You’ve been all I have for almost 10 years
at this point. I—”

Her arms swing around me, pulling me into a tight hug. She squeezes me, making it hard to breathe. I whimper under her tension. “I love you, Imbra.” Her arms tightened around me.

“I love you too.” I reply, my voice tight. I return her hug, letting her hold me. I can tell she needs it, so I try not to let her know she’s hurting me.

We sit together, holding each other for a few minutes. I struggle to breathe through every minute, but her comfort is important right now. This is the closest I’ve seen her to being present in a moment, I don’t
want to let it slip.

She finally lets go of me, moving her hands to my shoulders. “I’m sorry.” She says softly.

“What for?” I tilt my head. 

She stares at my nose blankly, thinking for a moment. “... Wasting your time.”

“No time with you is time wasted, Adva.” I respond, a small smile rising. Her gaze fixates on me, head hanging low. “Sit with me. You need the silence.”

I inch over to make room for her in my seat. Slowly, she sits next to me, her eyes scanning my desk. I realize at this moment just how long it’s been. 8 grueling years of fleeing from war, death, and hunger.
The one constant in my life throughout that time was Adva. No matter the location, the weather, or the situation, I could always find her face closeby. I’d be damned if I left her. 

“Thank you,” she uttered softly. The wind calmed, its temperature rising with its proximity to me. “I needed this.”

“And you still do.” I respond, mimicking her soft tone. “Let the silence hold you.”

I position the book in such a way that’s better for her, allowing her to read. Occasionally, she asks questions about the contents— most of which I don’t know the answer to, which brings intellectual conversations.
She even laughs a few times at some of my logical mistakes, each one being refreshing to hear. The Adva I know is still in there somewhere, but it doesn’t matter to me. Any Adva is my Adva.

The device Amei gave me— which had been sitting on the corner of the desk— vibrates. Adva reaches over to grab it, revealing a text message reading “We are leaving soon, tell Adva and Vael to prepare their belongings.”

I look over at Adva. “I’ll tell Kael.” She says, standing. “... Thank you again, Imbra. I don’t know what I’d do without you.”

“You don’t have to thank me.” I smile, standing as well. “Consider it… sister bonding time.”

She smiles back at me, before exiting my room, softly closing the door behind her. As per Amei’s request, I begin to pack my things, beginning first with my book and clothes. Then I grab small things that were here
when we arrived- a pencil from my desk, a printed picture of us three with Amei, and a small map of Gaea that Amei gave me. 

I step to the door, turning around to admire the room. I had been here for a while now, growing quite connected to the soft bedding and steady food. Everything here felt so stable and calm. I never had to worry about
surprise attacks, or soldier patrols. I’ll miss that feeling.

Entering the still messy living room, I find nobody. I decide to wait for them, sitting on the couch. The television is working again, but it’s on an odd channel that won’t change. All it says now is “How to comply
correctly” repeatedly, with the same 20-ish steps being repeated over and over again accompanied by animated sprites of humans arresting elementals. Oddly enough, the entire broadcast is accompanied by upbeat music,
even when some of the steps read “Let harm be done”, or “Don’t dare flourish”. 

I turn my head away from the evil broadcast. It felt taunting to see the elemental sprites drawn so weak and small compared to the humans. I just wonder where Amei is, and if she’s coming quickly. She said she wouldn’t 
leave. 

Kael and Adva come down the hallway together, talking to each other about food. They even laugh and joke about humans, calling them “oddly large” and “val-less”. Their insults are kind of amusing to me, but I don’t laugh.
I let them have their moment, I haven’t seen them connect like this since the camp.

“You know,” Kael says, sitting by the kitchen island. “I feel like the danger is massively overblown. We’re Elementals, we can definitely take them.”

Adva tilts her head. “I had the same thought, too.” She pauses, taking a glance over at me. “... But it’s better to be careful.”

Kael laughs. “Yeah. For sure.” Adva rolls her eyes.

Kael approaches the television, pressing buttons on the back and bottom of it, trying everything to change from the derogatory broadcast. “Stupid propaganda.” He utters. “They wish they could handle us like this.”

Amei’s still not here… I start to consider the worst. What if it wasn’t even her who sent the message? What if they’re trying to lure us in early before we can escape? I start making back-up plans. Whatever happens, I have
to be quick to strike. I can’t let them be quicker.

I remember what I read in my father’s book. If you don’t know how to fight, strike with your body. Close distances, use your shoulders and elbows, protect your head and neck. I rehearse it in my head about 5 times, imagining
a situation where I'd have to use the advice. 

I turn to Adva and Kael, interrupting their conversation. “Remember. If we have to fight… strike with your body.”

Kael tilts his head. “I read the book too, you know.” 

“She’s just worried.” Adva approaches me. “We’ll be fine, Imbra.” 

I know she doesn’t believe that, but I don’t say anything. She’s just trying to reassure me, after all. “... Okay.” I say, after a pause. “But… just remember it, just in case.”

Kael sticks his thumb up. “Alright.” Adva says, walking laps around the couch. 

After more waiting, Amei finally barges through the door. “Come on… let’s go.” She barely says, half-way out of breath. We all exit, approaching her vehicle, which she leans against waiting for us. She pants for oxygen,
leaning forward onto her knees, her face gleaming with sweat. 

When I go to approach her, she puts one of her hands up. Then she points to the back of the vehicle, almost shooing me away. Not wanting to upset her, I go to the back with Kael and Adva. I hope she’s okay.

All three of us jump into the spacious back of the vehicle. I hear the front door slam shut as Amei enters the driver's seat. Once we close the back doors, the vehicle revs quickly, the sudden momentum causing all three
of us to fall. I hear Adva and Kael grunt as they fail to secure their positions in the vehicle, being tossed around with me. 

“Amei! Slow down!” Kael yells.

“I will in a moment!” She yells back. “Please, just try to hold still!”

She’s truly wasting no time. Something must be happening— or already happened— for her to be acting like this. I end up holding onto Kael for stability, curled together in a corner with Adva in the one opposite us. The 
bouncing and sudden jerks of the vehicle almost knock us out of our spots, but we hold steady until Amei slows down.

“Are you alright?” She asks.

“Barely!” Adva responds quickly. 

I snicker. “We’re fine, Amei!” I yell back. I trust her to take care of us, even with what just happened. In fact, I prefer her to move fast. Whatever it takes to get us away from the danger. To be honest, I don’t even
want to know what it is. 

The ride is relatively smooth now. Though she’s still going somewhat fast, it’s not fast enough to throw us out of our seats. We get time to relax, talk, and even joke with her, talking about how life is on Gaea. She even
told us that humans call Gaea ‘Earth’. Gaea’s the better name by far, but I guess it’s their home. I wonder if they call Tulia something different.

Amei turns on music for us. It’s Gaean music, and we can’t understand any of the words, but we still sway to the rhythm. Amei translates some of the lyrics for us, some parts repeating “Memories are eternal”, which is
actually a common saying on Tulia. 

Suddenly, it all goes bright. A loud, ear piercing explosion sends flames, ash, and debris into the sky as the vehicle tumbles with us in it. I can’t find anything to cling to- my eyes and ears are failing me. 

I feel my head smash against metal as my hands claw at cushions. Cuts and bruises appear on my hands, and I feel dizzy. As my hearing and sight comes back, I hear the yelps of terror and pain from the others. Though my sight
has returned, the ash clouds prevent me from seeing. 

I mentally brace for another crash, but nothing moves. The vehicle has landed on its side, all three of us laying on what used to be the wall. I hear the sounds of raging flames burning outside the vehicle, the smog of
which finding its way into our space. 

The doors swing open, Amei’s bleeding face and limping body standing before it. She extends her hands out to us, which Kael and Adva grab before me. She pulls them both out before assisting me. 

“Are you all okay?” She asks, extending her hands to us all again. “Don’t move, I’ll—”

BANG.

BANG.

Her blood splatters on my body. Her face is mutilated by the weapon. She falls forward, her body barely missing us. My entire body goes stiff. That sound was too familiar— it’s the weapon.

I collapse to my knees, choking on my words. I reach my hands out to Amei, but it’s no use. My flames cannot undo this. I was too slow to protect her when she needed me most.

The footsteps of her murderers approach. My entire body goes stiff after my head turns to them. There’s six, all armed with the same weapon. All six of them pointing at us. All six of them yelling in the language I can’t
understand. 

I hear an enraged growl from Kael to my right. Before I can even look at him, he’s already charging at them. Adva and I both reach out to stop him—

BANG.

Kael falls back, his snow flying outward.

No.

I rush over to him, quicker than I had ever moved. My knees grow weak as tears well in my eyes. I hear the boots crunch ever closer, their language still pestering my ears. My hands hover over Kael’s body, blood rushing
out of him. I don’t know what to do. I don’t know what I can do. I hold his head in my hands, shaking him slightly. His element still flows, so he’s not dead— but he could be getting there. 

The wind stirs as Adva hyperventilates. Tears roll down my face as I turn to her. She stands subtly, in a non-threatening position as she raises her quivering hands into the air. It’s not like her to surrender… I hope
she has a plan.

Kael coughs, causing me to snap my head back to him. His yelps and groans of pain shatter me, further proving how useless I am. I keep holding him, trying to think of something— anything, I can do to make this situation
better. 

I feel cold metal press into the back of my head. My entire body goes stiff, my torso quivering as I hold my breath. They grab me, pulling me away from Kael. That’s when I resist, digging my feet and hands into the ground. 

The wind jerks, harder than I ever thought possible. All six of the humans stumble over onto the ground, me going with them. Using the opportunity, I run out of their grasp back over to Kael. On the way, I see Adva 
commanding the wind beautifully, her hands swaying through the air. 

I pull Kael behind the still burning vehicle for safety. Then Adva arrives with us—

BANG.

They keep ringing out, the vehicle’s metal shaking and clinking as they strike it. We all cower behind the safety, not knowing how safe it truly is. We could be moments away from death. That’s when it happens- the dart
returns. It forms in my quivering hand in perfect shape, ready to be thrown. 

Amei’s blood is still warm on my face. Her voice still rings through my mind. Kael still groans by my side. His blood still pours onto the ground. I can’t let them get away with this. 

I don’t even hesitate. The moment I hear a break in their steady bangs, I jump up and throw the dart at the first one I see, another dart replacing it as soon as it’s thrown. 

BOOM.

My target, and the humans immediately next to him, are immediately engulfed by massive raging flames. An ash cloud covers their eyes as the fires spread, burning their skin quicker than normal flames. The remaining three
humans panic, rushing to cover.

Again, I do not hesitate. I lob the new dart at another group of two, an even larger explosion coating them in the flames. Adva, who had stood with me, begins spreading the flames using the wind, catching the last two 
fleeing humans in my fiery grasp. 

Their screams are almost painful to hear. But then I remind myself what they did. What they would’ve done to me. I can’t feel bad for them. 

I step out from behind the vehicle, approaching the now burning corpses. One human had survived, his arms severely injured by my flames. My entire body quivers as I step closer to him, each step making him jump as he
tries to crawl away.

He leans on a nearby tree, staring up at me directly in my eyes. I hear the terror in his voice as he pleads to me in his human language. Another dart appears in my hand. I glance down at it, weighing my options, his
tone growing desperate.

I stare down at him for a moment, examining him. His face is slightly melted from my flames, but I can still see the glimmer from his tears. Every slight movement I make causes him to flinch massively, his weeping growing
louder.

I drop the dart by my side. The dart would be too merciful.

At this moment, I only feel rage. Hatred. Nothing could’ve convinced me that this human deserved life.

I kneel down before him as he tries his hardest to make any distance between us. Grabbing him by the leg, I tug him back, his face hovering just below my other hand. Then, after letting him linger in his terror, I grab his
face, releasing my energy directly into his face. 

His muffled screams are terrifying.

 Steam rises from his face as my hand sears his flesh. Slowly, his legs stop kicking. His screams stop rising. His arms stop twitching. When I remove my hand, he’s unrecognizable. No features, no skin, no hair. Nothing to
remember him by.

When I turn around, I see Adva standing there, motionless. The wind is even still. She stares directly at me, a slight hint of terror behind her eyes. 

“... Imbra.” She pauses, her voice low and shaking. “I… don’t know what to say.”

I walk to her, her eyes desperately fixated on me. She doesn’t even blink. “They’re the enemy, right?” I say softly. My voice shakes, too. “They would’ve done the same to us.”

I continue to walk, going past her. I step over one of the burned, lifeless bodies of the humans, their weapons laying on the ground. I need to check on Kael.

I kneel before him, his eyes looking up at me. 

“I’m okay.” He says, almost instantly. His voice is tight, his breath shuttering between words. I reach out to touch his shoulder. “It hardly hurts, really.”

“Don’t lie to me.” I respond quickly. When I touch his wounded shoulder, he recoils. 

“Okay, maybe it hurts a little.” A small smile rises on his face. 

I nod. “I know. How do you feel?”

He wipes a tear off my cheek, smearing Amei’s blood as his thumb moves across my face. “Better, now that you’re here.” 

“Not the time, Kael.” I say, shaking my head. 

Kael sniffled. “... Your hand smells—”

“I killed him.” I interrupted him. “Melted his face right off with my hand.” His screams ring through my mind.

He stares at me. I avoid his gaze, instead trying to fix up his clothes. “... You… burned his face off?” I nod. I can tell he wants to say more, but can’t figure himself out.

Adva comes to us, holding both of our bags. “I got our things.” Her voice still shakes, her eyes avoiding me entirely. “... What now?”

I help Kael up. Before I can even respond, I hear more footsteps approaching. “A slaughter here.” Xor’s voice called from behind me. I watched Adva’s expression change. “Proud of you, nascent.”

I turn, keeping my eyes away from his. “The human deserved it.” He utters. “Never tell yourself otherwise.”

“What now?” I ask softly, still keeping my eyes away from him. I don’t want to cooperate with him, but we hardly have many other choices. It’s either that, or be lost. 

“We continue Amei’s plan.” He kneels before Kael, examining his wound. “We fly to Antarctica, the coldest continent on this planet.”

I watch his hands closely. Kael flinches “How long will that take?” He asks, his breath still shaking.

“Optimally? 3 minutes.” Xor responds. He glances up at Adva for a moment, before returning his gaze to Kael’s wound. “Let’s not wait.” He picks Kael up, preparing for flight. “Stay close behind me. You don’t want to get 
lost.”

I look over at Adva, who sighs. She reluctantly prepares to fly. I do so as well, not wanting to let them leave without me. Following Xor, we take to the skies, the memory of the whole event flying with us. 

The entire flight is silent. The screams ring through my mind. The pain they must’ve felt being burnt alive. I don’t regret killing them… yet, maybe I will one day. But as of now? They deserved it. They got what was coming
to them. They killed Amei, and they would’ve killed us if we gave them the chance. 

Adva flies close to me. She doesn’t say anything, but her eyes avoid Xor’s position religiously. She flies in such a way that I am in between them, but she could still speak to me. And yet, she doesn’t. She lingers in the 
silence with me.

Once we arrive in Antarctica, we land in snowy plains of endless ice. It reminds me of Tulia’s Far South Continent, where the Snai and Icik live. Ice and snow as far as the eye can see. And worst of all? It’s freezing cold. 

When we land, Adva still stands close to me. Xor lays Kael in the snow, and begins using it to cover his wound. I don’t think it heals him, but it definitely numbs his pain.

“... I never thought you’d do something like that, Imbra.” Adva says softly.

I turn to her. “Kill someone?” 

She nods. “I always kind of saw you as… well, my little sister.” She chuckles slightly, though it sounds forced. “It felt… odd.”

“I’m still Imbra.” I smile. “Just with a kill count now.”

“Yeah… Imbra with a kill count.” She breaks her gaze away from me, a small smile rising on her face. “That’s certainly a way to think about it.”

“They would’ve killed us if they didn’t lose the authority.” I say quickly. “Mercy was not an option.”

She nods. “Yeah, no, it's just…” she pauses. “Don’t lose yourself.”

I struggle to form a response, words tripping over themselves in my mind. She looks over at me, expecting one. “... Okay.” I nod. “I won’t.”

“You can’t promise that.” She sits in the snow, carefully watching Xor. “But I appreciate it.”

I sit next to her, letting silence return. I lean into her shoulder with my own, the comfort and warmth bringing a sense of peace, despite the circumstances. For once, I feel peaceful. Despite the environment, I feel at home. 

“Ah, Miss Hayes!” A booming voice from the sky calls. An elemental hovers down, flaming, followed by two other, similar figures. “Irision calls your name. Do you answer?”



