There they were, three knights before us. Their uniforms were decked in the gold-orange trim of Irision, and their flames danced in deliberate yet peculiar ways. I could sense their power, just from the proximity. 

They stepped closer to us, each inch closer causing my heart to race faster. Adva and I retreated just as quickly as they approached, sticking by each other’s side. I hear the movement of Kael and Xor behind 
me, preparing for the worst.

“I asked you a question, Miss Hayes.” The knight says quickly. “I don’t usually repeat myself.”

My blood boils with rage. These are the same people who took my home. These people are the reason I’m no longer on Tulia. These people are the reason so many of my kind are dead. My entire body begins to shake 
with hatred, as I stop taking steps backwards.

The Knight giggles. “Not talkative, are we?” She continues walking to me, ordering the knights behind her to stop. Once she stands directly before me, she leans forward, her face landing mere inches in front 
of mine. 

The stare down between us grows tense once her smile fades. I watch as her eyes examine every inch of my face, from the flickers of my flames to the colors in my eye. Meanwhile, all I see is a monster, a demon
sent to destroy, kill, and plunder.

She finally turns to her knights. “Pick which one you want to kill.” She turns back to me. “Miss Hayes is mine. And make sure Xor’s death is painful.” 

The knights march towards Adva, Kael, and Xor. I don’t even get to question how they knew his name. My mind panics as the two knights march past me. I don’t want them to hurt my friends, but I’m under equal 
threat myself.

The knight in front of me sticks her hand out. “They call me Asli.” I don’t touch her. She drops her hand. “Rude.” 

“Fuck you.” I finally utter. “Go to hell, demon.”

“So you can talk.” She nods, rolling her lips inwards. “Sounds like someone needs to teach you manners.”

I can’t contain my hatred any longer. This freak followed me from Tulia searching for conflict. Why me? Why us? How dare they even invest time into such a thing? It’s not only cruel, it’s insulting. 

I finally make my move, punching her directly in her cheek as hard as I can. Her recovery was swift, only laughing at me. “Guess your father didn’t teach you how to fight?” She shakes her head. “Let’s get 
this over with, then.”

Before I could even blink, she grabs me by the neck and slams me into the snow. Then she flies low to the ground, still holding me, dragging me through the cold ground. The friction causes my flames to dampen
as my skin burns. 

She lets me go, allowing me to slide a little further, before she appears above me, crushing her feet directly into my chest. I yelp in pain as lungs struggle beneath my fiery skin, her smug smile above me 
remaining ever taunting.

“It’s in there somewhere, Miss Hayes.” She pushes her foot further into my chest, causing me to gasp for any air as tears well in my eyes. I helplessly punch and scratch at her ankles. “We’ll release the real
you today.”

I can’t even respond. Darts try and fail to form in my hands as my measly attempts to fight back are swiftly ignored. Once she takes her foot off of me, I inhale massively. Each move I make has my body screaming,
begging me to stop. Regardless, I stand to my feet. 

Asli laughs. “You’re persistent! Exactly what we need for new Knights.” She rolls her shoulders, small cracking noises coming from them. “Come on, just surrender, and join us.”

I pant, staring at her as she waits for my response. “Fuck you.” I say, gasping between the words.

“You’re really going to make me kill you, aren’t you?” She walks towards me. I miss another punch, and she responds with a jab directly to my neck. I struggle to breathe as she grabs me by my hair, forcing me 
to look at her. “You’ll die, Miss Hayes. Surrender.”

I spit in her face, and kick her thigh. Both of which seem to upset her more than they hurt her. After her eyebrows furrow, I feel her knee jam directly into my chest, causing me to tumble quickly through the snow
again. Nothing I do slows my roll, but she manages to grab me by the hair again, before slamming me back into the ground, leaving a small crater where I impacted.

I barely manage to get on all fours, my heart struggling as my hearing begins to fail. Asli kicks me directly in my face. “Disrespectful fool.” she utters as she drags me into the sky, before slamming me back down
at full speed. I feel my bones crack and muscles tear, causing me to scream from the excruciating pain. 

Then she steps on my neck, pushing down as hard as she can without instantly severing my head. I struggle, scratching and kicking to try and escape. Her empty eyes stare me down as my vision blurs and I grow weak,
my flames slowly retreating inward. 

I can only feel my rage. I can only feel my fear. I can only feel my hatred. At this moment, all I can do is think. People like her took my home. People like her took my parents. I can’t let them take me. I have
to try harder. I will try harder.

I gather my courage and dignity, Asli’s boot still firm on my neck. I push her off and stand on my feet as quick as I can, ignoring my pain, then fly directly at her, tackling her to the ground and giving her the 
treatment she gave me. I drag her through the snow, scratching and punching her face as I do. 

Then I stomp on her chest, leaving a small crater beneath her. The darts begin to form in my hand, and I lob them at her as quickly as they come, leaving explosions where she rests. I land nearby, panting and 
exhausted.

Once the smoke clears, I see her there kneeling. Blood drips from her lips. “Hm.” She utters, as she touches her blood. “Fine. Let’s get flashy.”

A lasso of flames wraps around me, pulling me towards her as her first slams into my face. Then she uppercuts me, sending me flying into the sky, before yanking me back down with the lasso and kicking me away. 

I try to wiggle out of the lasso, failing to ignore all the pain. I see her jump slightly before slamming her foot into the ground, causing a pillar of fire to engulf me, burning me with my own element. Using the
lasso still attached to me, she pulls me down directly into her fist, and—

…

It’s all gone again. No sound. No sight. No pain. Not even thoughts, this time. 

The darkness is replaced by a bright light. I’m overwhelmed by endless bliss as all of my anxiety and pain disappears, even if for just a moment. A peaceful note hums in my ears nonstop as my body feels as if it
floats in the ocean. I can’t even remember why I was upset.

Then I hear it. A voice, unfamiliar and alien.

“You’re not done burning yet.”

It all gets torn away. I remember why I’m enraged. I feel my pain. The world returns to my senses, but my pain is purged shortly after. I feel my bones and muscles force themselves back into place as I feel a burst
of energy. New energy, stronger than the val I had previously, pushes its way throughout my entire body.

Asli nods. “There it is.” 

My body feels lighter and my flames burn hotter. A new found confidence pushes me to consider moves I wouldn’t have a few moments ago. So I build my own initiative, this new power giving me more energy than I know
what to do with.

I move faster than I could even process, deciding to get creative. When I land my first hit, I feel my energy transfer directly into her flesh, causing her to stumble. 

Oddly enough, it begins to feel fun. As she tumbles, I land on top of her directly, pressing my feet into her and sliding her across the ground, the friction causing snow and ice around us to melt. When we lose
all of our momentum, I kick her up into the air, then blast her with flames directly from my palm, the force of which sending her flying.

 This is the perfect opportunity to test my speed! I dig my hands into the ground and launch myself towards her, but she catches me by the head, covering my face.

Her fist lands directly below my ribcage, the sheer force of the impact causing the snow and ice around us to melt. I get sent flying through the air, the moment lasting too long before I feel my body drag against
the ground, leaving a clear trail through the snow.

I stand to my feet, my body fighting back. Begging me to stay down. But the force within demands I fight. The energy coursing through my veins demands release. I search around for my target, spinning in circles. 

When I finally find her, it’s too late. She’s already striking me again, this time into the air. I hover for a moment, giving me the opportunity to recover, only to turn around and see her— a storm of raging flames 
shadowing her figure as she floats above me, replacing the sun. She hits me again, this time directly in my nose, sending me tumbling through the air back to the ground. 

The pain is too much. Despite trying to stand, my injuries make it impossible. I look up at her as she takes slow steps towards me, the storm of fire following behind her. When she finally reaches me, she just waits.
She stares down upon me. I feel her gaze pierce my soul, her expectations weighing down on me.

After a while staring at me, she sighs before picking me up by my neck. I can hardly put up a resistance. She begins punching me, each hit landing on my nose. The continuous strikes cause blood to gush out of my face
as my senses slowly fail. 

Snow covers us, causing us to fall to the ground. Asli stands quickly, spinning to find the cause of the attack. “Hey, asshole.” Kael calls. She snaps over to him. “I’m over here.”

No.

Asli chuckles. “And to whom do I owe the pleasure?”

“Kael.” He answers, after a pause. “Kael Voris.” I’m still trying to stand, but my body is overwhelmed by sensations of pain as I choke on my own blood. Please, no. I try to tell him to leave, but I can’t— I just cough
up blood in place of my words.

I watch as Asli slowly approaches him. “So eager to die for Miss Hayes.” She shakes her head. “You’re nothing, not even Firen. Whatever. I’ll crush you.”

I feel the heat of her dashing away in his direction. From there, I can only hear the chaos. Snow and flames flying, Kael’s yelps and screams of pain and terror, Asli’s taunting laughter. I push harder and harder, yet
my body only seems to push back. I have to do something. 

My flames flickered to a black and red color. My entire body shook with each scream from Kael. I felt my body become overrun by rage and hate. Suddenly, all my mind can focus on is Asli. Her face. Her laughter. Her
fire. My flames now drench in the hellish color as my body heals itself once again. 

Once I hear even a slight hint to her position, I dash fast enough to bring the snow I was standing on with me. Within an instant, I grab her by her neck and begin unleashing a flurry of darts, punches, and blasts of
fire directly onto her as I yell incoherently. 

A new dart manifests in my hands. A larger, sharper one, sharing my new red and black color. Without thinking, I use it to stab her repeatedly, over and over. When she pushes me off of her, the dart has been left in 
her neck.

She pulls the dart out, moaning in pain as she does, touching around the wound. Unlike her others, her fire doesn’t cover them immediately. It lingers, staring back at me.

I dash back to her again, kicking her in the head. Then I throw her in the air, and unleash a massive beam of flames in her direction. I watch as she falls to the ground, her neck gushing blood.

Each step I take melts snow around my foot, before I dash to her again, my knee landing directly on her chin. Then I pick her up by the collar of her shirt, and she watches as I charge my fist with energy.

She nods. “Let’s see how strong you are with that Hellfire.”

I impact, directly in her stomach. She goes flying, but I send a string of flames to catch her, pulling her right back— before I hit her with the exact same attack, this time aiming downwards so she hits the ground, 
the impact causing a crater where we stand. 

She coughs up blood on the ground, spitting out more right after. “Alright.” She rolls her neck and clears her throat. “My turn.” 

She speeds herself up as she rises, impacting my chin, sending me directly into the sky far above the clouds. By the time she appears above the clouds, I’ve already recovered from her strike. 

We clash once, pausing the fight for just long enough for me to catch her gaze. There is no fear behind them. I’ll give her something to fear. 

After a short trade of blows, she kicks me in the head with her heel, causing me to tumble in contact with the ground, fighting to catch myself with my hands in the snow, melting it as I slide. I give up on the ground,
instead using my flames to burst my way back to her, clashing with her thrice, causing more explosions. 

For a moment, we split, bringing downtime and silence. Kael uses the moment to approach me, limping as he holds his arm. “I can’t fight too well because of my arm…” He says softly, his voice thin. “But that won’t stop 
me from helping how I can.”

I stand, hovering slightly above the ground. “We’re killing her.” I utter, a low growl under my voice. I stare where she last was, waiting for her to reappear.

Kael glances at me. “Whatever you say.” He responds, through strained breaths.

The air rumbles as Asli flies. “The bug’s back for more?” She yells from the sky. “Watch how easy it is to melt snow.”

She crashes down from the sky, catching Kael as she goes by. Kael uses the surrounding snow to bury them. He slips his way out, right before I punch the mound, pushing Asli out of it. Then a flurry of darts chase her,
exploding once they get close enough. 

Asli defeats Kael and I using a fire whip, cracking through the air with quick and painful swings, making it hard to close the distance. She lands a crucial strike on the back of my legs, causing me to fall onto the
ground. I push myself up to all fours.

Suddenly, a boot hits the back of my head, knocking me straight to the ground. When I try to stand, it presses on the back of my neck, pushing me into the ground. “This must be hellfire.” A new voice called. A male,
raspy and rough. “The colors are beautiful. A shame you’re not on our side, nascent.” 

When I finally push the boot off of me, another one kicks me in my side, causing me to cough blood. “Retrieve Knightsi Irson, we’re leaving.” She kicks me again, and I growl in response, failing to conjure my flames.
“Miss Hayes is too volatile. Hellfire’s temperature is too high. But we’re leaving with something. Consider Xor.” 

I hear the flames of the stranger Knight flourish as he flies off. Asli picks me up, holding me by my hair. “One last chance.” I see Kael sneaking up on her from behind, limping as he charges an attack in his fist, 
snow rising and wrapping around his lower body. “We take you… or the Snai.” 

I spit in her face. She growls, and reaches to strike me— before pausing. She waits, listening. Hearing the snow crunch under Kael’s feet as he approaches. My breath thins. “Kael, no—”

She whips around, her hand slamming into Kael’s face. He falls to the ground, motionless. His element still flows, but it’s slowing down. I feel my blood boil as my rage grows, tears welling in my eyes. 

Asli turns back to me, punching me repeatedly in my face. Blood begins to gush out of my nose and lips, but the energy keeps me awake and focused. 

The other Knights arrive, floating behind me. “Be faster next time.” She drops me on the ground, discarding me like litter. “Pick up the Snai. That’s our prize for today.”

I hear them fly towards Kael. I try to push myself to stop them, but my body screams in pain once again. I crawl as close as I can to Kael, reaching out for his hand. I feel his cold fingers on mine, the contrast
helping to ground me for a moment. 

But they pull him away. They began to fly. I could only watch helplessly as the distance grew. All because of the evils of the world. All because of Knightsdales. All because of Asli. 

My rage grew as the energy enhanced itself multiple times over. This time, my body did not heal. In fact, it broke further, as massive wings of fire tore through my muscles and skin, sharing the same colors of my 
hellfire. 

I feel my senses fade— all but my vision and hearing. My mind repeats her name over and over. Asli. She must die. She can’t get away. 

I burst off the ground, my flaming wings flapping to give me speed. I yell with rage as I grow closer to the fleeing knights, who have reached the edge of this planet’s atmosphere. I watch as they reorganize, 
preparing to fight me. All I want is their blood on my skin. All I want is to save Kael.

Suddenly, they all stop flying at the same time. They charge down at me, each kicking me once, their momentum compared to mine causing me to fall back, stunned and dizzy. My wings hide the sun behind them as I cut
through the wind, hitting the ground on my back.

I feel empty, my entire body resting hollow, as I stare up at the sky. The wings disappear as my flames return to their normal colors, tears welling in my eyes again. I didn’t just fail, I lost someone I didn’t 
know I needed.

I am in pain, but my heart hurts more. I crawl in a random direction, weeping silently. If it weren’t for his injury, he would’ve been able to defend himself. I feel my throat tighten as my body weakens. I collapse
